|~ `::any`, the kind beneath every kind, and what is declared under it:
   the refusal of a host's value JSON has no form for, and the words
   every value answers.
   The decisions it exists for: [QSDSP#residence]. ~|

:any
  |~~ Any is the kind beneath every kind, where the words every value
      answers live: `type`, `eq`, `doc`, `print`, `json`, `explain`,
      `elide`, `if` and their kin. A word that means the same for every
      value is written once here, and the walk from a name to its verb
      ends here, after the kinds of the subject.

      No value is of this kind alone, since `type` answers the kind
      above it, and every value answers the words listed here.
      ::snippet~(::any | manifest) lists the words of every value.
      ::link~(::qlang/verbs | doc)
  ~~|
  ::kind
    | proves :holdsType
        |~~ `type` lives on `::any`. ~~|
        ~(::any | manifest | has ::any/type)
    | proves :isTheKindOfNoValue
        |~~ No value's type is `::any`. ~~|
        ~(5 | type | eq ::any | not)

:any/desc
  |~~ Answers the subject under `::desc`, `5 | desc` answering
      `::desc#5`, a value that ranks the greater first: a key of `sort`,
      `min` or `max` that answers it descends, `[1 3 2] | sort ~(desc)`
      answering `[3 2 1]`, and in a vector key it turns its own place
      alone, `sort ~(get [~(get /n | desc) /s])`. A value of every kind turns, a
      map among them, and `payload` reads it back. The key is code, a
      quote: `sort desc` turns the vector itself, which `sort` refuses
      as no key. A desc of a desc ranks as the value does, and a key
      turned in `max` finds the least. The call takes no value; a field
      turns by reading it first, `~(get /n | desc)`.
      ::link~(::desc | doc) ::link~(::vec/sort | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns
        |~~ The subject under `::desc`, which ranks the greater first. ~~|
        ::desc
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `desc` turns its subject and takes no value: a key turns a
            field by reading it first, `sort ~(get /n | desc)`.
        ~~|
    | proves :answersADesc
        |~~ `desc` answers the subject under `::desc`. ~~|
        ~(5 | desc | type | eq ::desc)
    | proves :descendsASort
        |~~ A sort by `desc` descends. ~~|
        ~([1 3 2] | sort ~(desc) | eq [3 2 1])
    | proves :turnsOnePlaceOfAVectorKey
        |~~ In a vector key `desc` turns its own place alone. ~~|
        ~([{:n 1 :s "b"} {:n 1 :s "a"} {:n 2 :s "c"}]
          | sort ~(get [~(get /n | desc) /s]) | map /s | eq ["c" "a" "b"])
    | proves :readsBackByPayload
        |~~ A map turns, and `payload` reads it back. ~~|
        ~({:n 1} | desc | payload | eq {:n 1})
    | proves :turnsTheWholeVectorWithoutAQuote
        |~~ `sort desc` turns the vector, which `sort` refuses as no key.
        ~~|
        ~([1 3 2] | sort desc
          !| get [~(type) /actualType]
          |  eq [::vec/sort/key/KindNotTakenError ::desc])
    | proves :ranksTurnedTwiceAsTheValue
        |~~ A desc of a desc ranks as the value does. ~~|
        ~([1 3 2] | sort ~(desc | desc) | eq [1 2 3])
    | proves :turnsAMaximum
        |~~ A key turned in `max` finds the least. ~~|
        ~([{:n 1} {:n 2}] | max ~(get /n | desc) | eq {:n 1})
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesAField
        |~~ A field given to `desc` is refused under the word. ~~|
        ~([{:n 1} {:n 2}] | sort ~(desc (get /n)) !| type | eq ::any/desc/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/eq
  |~~ Answers whether the subject and the value the call gives are one
      value: of one kind, under the same tags, and equal part for part,
      the order of a map's keys and of a set's elements aside. So a
      keyword is not its string, `:a` and `"a"`; a vector is not the set
      of its elements, `[1 2]` and `#[1 2]`; a value under a tag is not
      its payload; and a number equals itself whatever its digits, `1` and
      `1.0`, while a sum of doubles may miss, `0.1 | add 0.2`. The value
      is computed against the subject, so two fields of one record are
      compared through a name, `:r it | get /a | eq (r | get /b)`, and in
      a condition the subject is the element,
      `filter ~(get /status | eq "open")`. `ne` answers the opposite.
      ::link~(::any/ne | doc) ::link~(::qlang/values | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :value
        |~~ The value compared with the subject, any value, computed
            against the subject.
        ~~|
        /0
    | throws :value/NotGivenError
        |~~ `eq` compares the subject with the value its call gives,
            `5 | eq 5`; a call with no value is refused.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ `true` when the subject and the value are one value, `false`
            otherwise.
        ~~|
        ::boolean
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `eq` compares the subject with one value and was given more:
            whether the subject is one of several is
            `:x it | [5 6] | contains x`.
        ~~|
    | proves :comparesPartForPart
        |~~ Nested values compare part for part, a set without its order.
        ~~|
        ~({:a [1 #[2 3]]} | eq {:a [1 #[3 2]]})
    | proves :keepsAKeywordApartFromItsString
        |~~ A keyword is not its string. ~~|
        ~(:a | eq "a" | not)
    | proves :keepsAVectorApartFromItsSet
        |~~ A vector is not the set of its elements. ~~|
        ~([1 2] | eq #[1 2] | not)
    | proves :keepsATagOverItsPayload
        |~~ A value under a tag is not its payload. ~~|
        ~(::Box[1] | eq [1] | not)
    | proves :readsANumberByItsValue
        |~~ A number equals itself whatever its digits. ~~|
        ~(1 | eq 1.0)
    | proves :comparesDoublesAsTheyAdd
        |~~ A sum of doubles may miss the number it is written as. ~~|
        ~(0.1 | add 0.2 | eq 0.3 | not)
    | proves :comparesTwoFieldsThroughAName
        |~~ Two fields of one record compare through a name. ~~|
        ~({:a 1 :b 1} | :r it | get /a | eq (r | get /b))
    | proves :value/NotGivenError/refusesACallWithNoValue
        |~~ A call with no value is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~(5 | eq !| type | eq ::any/eq/value/NotGivenError)
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesTwoValues
        |~~ A call with two values is refused under the word. ~~|
        ~(5 | eq 5 6 !| type | eq ::any/eq/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | proves :keepsTheOrderOfKeysAside
        |~~ Two maps of the same entries are one value, whatever the order
            of their keys.
        ~~|
        ~({:a 1 :b 2} | eq {:b 2 :a 1})
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/ne
  |~~ Answers whether the subject and the value the call gives differ, by
      the equality of `eq`, whose opposite it is: a keyword differs from
      its string, a vector from the set of its elements, a value under a
      tag from its payload.
      ::link~(::any/eq | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :value
        |~~ The value compared with the subject, any value, computed
            against the subject.
        ~~|
        /0
    | throws :value/NotGivenError
        |~~ `ne` compares the subject with the value its call gives,
            `5 | ne 6`; a call with no value is refused.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ `true` when the subject and the value differ, `false` when
            they are one value.
        ~~|
        ::boolean
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `ne` compares the subject with one value, `5 | ne 6`; a call
            with more is refused.
        ~~|
    | proves :answersTheOppositeOfEq
        |~~ Two values differ exactly when they are not one value. ~~|
        ~([[1 2] [1 1] [:a "a"]] | map ~(:p it | get /0 | ne (p | get /1)) | eq [true false true])
    | proves :value/NotGivenError/refusesACallWithNoValue
        |~~ A call with no value is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~(5 | ne !| type | eq ::any/ne/value/NotGivenError)
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesTwoValues
        |~~ A call with two values is refused under the word. ~~|
        ~(5 | ne 6 7 !| type | eq ::any/ne/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/type
  |~~ Answers the kind of the subject, the tag its value stands under,
      `5 | type` answering `::number`. The answer is a tag name, a value
      that reads the kind's page, `type | doc`, and compares,
      `filter ~(type | eq ::string)`. Every value answers: a literal its
      kind, `"hi"`, `:foo`, `[1 2]` and `#[1 2]` answering `::string`,
      `::keyword`, `::vec` and `::set`; null `::null`; a quote, a doc and
      a tag name `::quote`, `::doc` and `::tag`. A value under tags
      answers the outermost, `::Width#5` answering `::Width`, while `is`
      walks the tags to the kind beneath. A failure passes over the
      step; `!|` catches it, and the error at rest answers the tag of the
      refusal it carries. The call takes no value: whether the subject is of a kind
      is `is`.
      ::link~(::any/is | doc) ::link~(::any/tag | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The kind of the subject, its outermost tag. ~~| ::tag
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `type` answers the kind of its subject and takes no value:
            whether the subject is of a kind is `is`, `5 | is ::number`.
        ~~|
    | proves :ofANumber
        |~~ A number literal is a number. ~~|
        ~(42 | type | eq ::number)
    | proves :readsThePageOfItsKind
        |~~ The answer reads the page of the kind. ~~|
        ~(5 | type | doc | eq (::number | doc))
    | proves :comparesAsATagName
        |~~ The answer compares with a tag name. ~~|
        ~(["a" 1 "b"] | filter ~(type | eq ::string) | eq ["a" "b"])
    | proves :ofAString
        |~~ A string literal is a string. ~~|
        ~("hi" | type | eq ::string)
    | proves :ofAKeyword
        |~~ A keyword literal is a keyword. ~~|
        ~(:foo | type | eq ::keyword)
    | proves :ofAVector
        |~~ A vector literal is a vector. ~~|
        ~([1 2] | type | eq ::vec)
    | proves :ofASet
        |~~ A set literal is a set. ~~|
        ~(#[1 2] | type | eq ::set)
    | proves :ofNull
        |~~ Null answers `::null`, a missing field read by `query` among
            them.
        ~~|
        ~({} | query /a | type | eq ::null)
    | proves :ofAQuote
        |~~ A quote answers `::quote`. ~~|
        ~(~(42) | type | eq ::quote)
    | proves :ofADoc
        |~~ A doc answers `::doc`. ~~|
        ~(|~~ A page. ~~| | type | eq ::doc)
    | proves :ofATagName
        |~~ A tag name answers `::tag`. ~~|
        ~(::vec | type | eq ::tag)
    | proves :answersTheOutermostTag
        |~~ A value under tags answers the outermost. ~~|
        ~(::Width#5 | type | eq ::Width)
    | proves :readsAnErrorOnceOpened
        |~~ An error passes over `type`, and the error at rest answers
            the tag of its refusal.
        ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 | type !| type | eq ::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError)
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesAKind
        |~~ A kind given to `type` is refused under the word. ~~|
        ~(5 | type ::number !| type | eq ::any/type/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/json
  |~~ Answers the subject written as JSON text, for a reader that does
      not read qlang: `{:a 1 :b 2} | json` answers `{"a":1,"b":2}`.
      Every value answers, and the shapes of qlang alone give way at
      this boundary: a keyword writes as its bare name, a key and a
      value alike; a set as an array; a value under a tag as an object
      of its `$tag` and its `payload`, an error at rest among them; a
      quote, a doc and a tag name as
      the string of their print. So `parseJson` reads a keyword back as
      its string and a set as a vector: the text that reads back as the
      value is `print`. The call takes no value, an indent among them.
      ::link~(::any/print | doc) ::link~(::string/parseJson | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The JSON text of the subject. ~~| ::string
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `json` writes the whole subject on one line and takes no
            value, an indent among them: a field is written by reading it
            first, `get /a | json`.
        ~~|
    | proves :writesAMapAsAnObject
        |~~ A map is written as an object. ~~|
        ~({:a 1 :b 2} | json | eq "{\"a\":1,\"b\":2}")
    | proves :writesKeywordsAsNames
        |~~ A keyword is written as its bare name. ~~|
        ~([:foo :bar] | json | eq "[\"foo\",\"bar\"]")
    | proves :writesAnErrorAtRestUnderItsTag
        |~~ An error at rest is written as a value under its tag. ~~|
        ~(::error{:k 1} | json | parseJson | keys | eq #[:"$tag" :payload])
    | proves :writesASetAsAnArray
        |~~ A set is written as an array. ~~|
        ~(#[1 2 3] | json | eq "[1,2,3]")
    | proves :writesATaggedValueAsAnObject
        |~~ A value under a tag is written as an object of its tag and its
            payload.
        ~~|
        ~(::Box{:a 1} | json | eq "{\"$tag\":\"Box\",\"payload\":{\"a\":1}}")
    | proves :writesAQuoteAsItsPrint
        |~~ A quote is written as the string of its print. ~~|
        ~(~(add 1) | json | eq "\"~(add 1)\"")
    | proves :readsBackAsStringsAndVectors
        |~~ `parseJson` reads a keyword back as its string and a set as a
            vector.
        ~~|
        ~({:k :foo :s #[1 2]} | json | parseJson | eq {:k "foo" :s [1 2]})
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesAnIndent
        |~~ An indent given to `json` is refused under the word. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | json 2 !| type | eq ::any/json/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/print
  |~~ Answers the text that reads back as the subject, `42 | print`
      answering `"42"`, the text the command line shows for an answer
      other than a string, which it shows as it is. An atom prints as
      its literal, a string inside its quotes; a vector and a map by
      their brackets around the print of each part; a set, a quote, a
      doc and an error by brackets of their own; any other value under a
      tag as its tag before the print of its payload. A set prints in
      its one order, and the text reads back as the value by `parse`
      and `apply`. A kind may answer a `print` of its own declared under
      it, `:Box/print`, and the print of a value takes it for each part
      of that kind. The call takes no value: a print within a budget is
      `elide 80 | print`.
      ::link~(::any/json | doc) ::link~(::any/elide | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The literal the subject prints as. ~~| ::string
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `print` writes the whole subject and takes no value: a print
            within a budget of characters is `elide 80 | print`.
        ~~|
    | proves :printsANumber
        |~~ A number prints as its literal. ~~|
        ~(42 | print | eq "42")
    | proves :printsAStringInsideItsQuotes
        |~~ A string prints inside its quotes. ~~|
        ~("x" | print | eq "\"x\"")
    | proves :printsATaggedValue
        |~~ A tagged value prints with its tag. ~~|
        ~(::builtin{:a 1} | print | eq "::builtin{:a 1}")
    | proves :printsASetInItsOrder
        |~~ A set prints in its one order. ~~|
        ~("#[2 1]" | parse | apply it | print | eq "#[1 2]")
    | proves :readsBack
        |~~ The text reads back as the value. ~~|
        ~({:a [1 "x"]} | :v it | print | parse | :q it | apply q | eq v)
    | proves :takesThePrintOfAKind
        |~~ The print of a value takes the `print` of a kind for each part
            of it.
        ~~|
        ~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~| | :Box/print ~("box") | [::Box[1] 2] | print | eq "[box 2]")
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesABudget
        |~~ A budget given to `print` is refused under the word. ~~|
        ~([1 2] | print 80 !| type | eq ::any/print/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/elide
  |~~ Answers the subject within a budget of characters of its print,
      what does not fit given way to markers under `::elision`, and a
      value whose print fits as it is. The largest part gives way first,
      or in its place a part within it that prints more than half of it:
      a string, a doc, a quote or a map beneath the top gives way whole,
      the marker of a text keeping its first characters under `:head`; a
      vector or a set keeps the first half of its elements, and half of
      those until it fits, ending with a marker of the rest, a set
      standing in the one order; a value under a tag keeps its tag, its
      parts giving way within; and a part a kind prints its own way
      stands as that kind prints it, the budget counting that print. The
      top keeps its shape: a vector or a set there keeps its first
      element, a map gives way within, and a string, a doc, a quote or an
      atom alone is answered whole. A part gives way only where the least
      it gives way to prints shorter than it, `[]` or `{:n 1}` staying. A
      marker's `:size` counts what it stands for, a text by its
      characters as `count` counts them, and its `:read`, applied to the
      value `elide` took,
      answers the part left out. The call gives the budget, a number; the
      command line applies `elide` to every answer it prints when
      `--budget=N` asks it to.
      ::link~(::elision | doc) ::link~(::any/print | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :budget
        |~~ The budget, in characters of the print. ~~|
        /0 ::number
    | throws :budget/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The budget is a number of characters, `elide 80`. ~~|
    | throws :budget/NotGivenError
        |~~ `elide` keeps the subject within the budget the call gives,
            the characters of its print, `elide 80`.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ The subject within the budget, its parts that did not fit
            given way to markers.
        ~~|
        ::any
    | proves :leavesWhatFits
        |~~ A value within the budget stays whole. ~~|
        ~([1 2] | elide 100 | eq [1 2])
    | proves :elidesTheLargestPart
        |~~ The largest part gives way to an elision. ~~|
        ~({:s "a long line of text to leave out, which runs on long enough that its marker prints shorter than it" :n 1}
          | elide 40 | get /s | type | eq ::elision)
    | proves :keepsTheHeadOfAString
        |~~ The marker of a string keeps its first characters. ~~|
        ~({:s "a long line of text to leave out, which runs on long enough that its marker prints shorter than it" :n 1}
          | elide 40 | get /s/head | eq "a long line of text to leave out, which ")
    | proves :keepsAPartShorterThanItsMarker
        |~~ A part whose marker prints no shorter than it stays. ~~|
        ~([[] {:n 1} "short"] | elide 3 | eq [[] {:n 1} "short"])
    | proves :countsATextByItsCharacters
        |~~ The marker of a text counts and keeps characters as `count`
            and `take` do.
        ~~|
        ~(61 | range | map ~("😀") | join "" | :s it | {} | put /s s | elide 30 | get /s | get [/size ~(get /head | count)] | eq [61 30])
    | proves :halvesAVectorUntilItFits
        |~~ A vector keeps its first elements and ends with a marker of
            the rest.
        ~~|
        ~(["alpha" "bravo" "charlie" "delta" "echo" "foxtrot" "golf" "hotel" "india" "juliet"]
          | elide 80
          | eq ["alpha" "bravo" ::elision{:size 8 :read ~(drop 2)}])
    | proves :keepsATag
        |~~ A value under a tag keeps its tag. ~~|
        ~(::Box{:s "a long line of text to leave out, which runs on long enough that its marker prints shorter than it"}
          | elide 10 | type | eq ::Box)
    | proves :keepsTheFirstElementOfTheTop
        |~~ The top keeps its first element, the part within it giving way.
        ~~|
        ~(200 | range | map ~("a") | join "" | :s it | get [~(s) ~(s)] | elide 200 | get [~(count) ~(last | type)] | eq [2 ::elision])
    | proves :halvesASetInTheOneOrder
        |~~ A set keeps the first half of its elements and stands in the one
            order.
        ~~|
        ~(40 | range | distinct | elide 60 | get [~(type) ~(last | type)] | eq [::set ::elision])
    | proves :givesADocWayWithTheHeadOfItsText
        |~~ A doc beneath the top gives way whole, its marker keeping the
            first characters of its text.
        ~~|
        ~(get [~(::vec/sort | doc)] | elide 100 | get /0/head | type | eq ::string)
    | proves :countsThePrintAKindGives
        |~~ A part a kind prints its own way stands as that kind prints it.
        ~~|
        ~(:Box |~~ B ~~| | :Box/print ~("b") | [::Box#1 ::Box#2 ::Box#3 ::Box#4 ::Box#5 ::Box#6 ::Box#7] | elide 10 | print | count | eq 15)
    | proves :readsTheElidedPartBack
        |~~ An elision holds the query that reads its part back. ~~|
        ~({:s "a long line of text to leave out, which runs on long enough that its marker prints shorter than it" :n 1}
          | :v it | elide 40 | get /s/read | :r it | v | apply r | eq (v | get /s))
    | proves :answersAStringWhole
        |~~ A string alone is answered whole. ~~|
        ~("a long line of text" | elide 4 | eq "a long line of text")
    | proves :budget/KindNotTakenError/refusesAString
        |~~ A budget that is no number is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~([1 2] | elide "x" !| type | eq ::any/elide/budget/KindNotTakenError)
    | proves :budget/NotGivenError/refusesACallWithNoBudget
        |~~ A call with no budget is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~([1 2] | elide !| type | eq ::any/elide/budget/NotGivenError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/if
  |~~ Answers the branch the condition chooses, run against the subject:
      `then` when the condition holds, `else` when it fails,
      `5 | if (gt 0) ~("positive") ~("negative")` answering `"positive"`.
      The condition is a boolean computed against the subject at the
      call, `if (get /age | gte 18)`; a branch is code, a quote, so only the
      chosen branch runs, and the empty quote `~()` leaves the subject
      as it is. So the condition is no quote, `if ~(gt 0)` being
      refused, and null and a number are refused too, having no second
      reading; a field the record lacks answers its error, which
      `query /flag | eq true` asks around. Under `map` the subject is
      each element, `map ~(if (gt 0) ~(it) ~(0))`. The call takes the
      condition and both branches, and a chain of conditions is `cond`.
      ::link~(::any/cond | doc) ::link~(::any/coalesce | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :condition
        |~~ The condition, a boolean computed against the subject at the
            call.
        ~~|
        /0 ::boolean
    | throws :condition/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The condition is a boolean computed at the call, `if (gt 0)`:
            a quote, `~(gt 0)`, is code and no boolean, and null or a
            number has no second reading.
        ~~|
    | takes :then
        |~~ The branch run when the condition holds, a quote. ~~|
        /1 #[::path ::quote]
    | throws :then/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The then branch is code, a quote, `~("yes")`, or a path, run
            only when chosen.
        ~~|
    | takes :else
        |~~ The branch run when the condition fails, a quote. ~~|
        /2 #[::path ::quote]
    | throws :else/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The else branch is code, a quote, `~("no")`, or a path, run
            only when chosen.
        ~~|
    | throws :else/NotGivenError
        |~~ `if` takes both branches: a branch that leaves the subject as
            it is is the empty quote, `if (gt 0) ~(add 1) ~()`.
        ~~|
    | returns |~~ The answer of the branch the condition chose. ~~| ::any
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `if` takes one condition and two branches: a chain of
            conditions is `cond`,
            `cond ~(gte 90) ~("A") ~(gte 80) ~("B") ~("C")`.
        ~~|
    | proves :runsThenWhenItHolds
        |~~ The then branch runs when the condition holds. ~~|
        ~(5 | if (gt 0) ~("positive") ~("non-positive") | eq "positive")
    | proves :runsElseWhenItFails
        |~~ The else branch runs when it does not. ~~|
        ~(-1 | if (gt 0) ~("positive") ~("non-positive") | eq "non-positive")
    | proves :readsTheSubject
        |~~ The condition reads the subject. ~~|
        ~({:age 25} | if (get /age | gte 18) ~("adult") ~("minor") | eq "adult")
    | proves :runsTheBranchOnTheSubject
        |~~ A branch runs against the subject. ~~|
        ~(5 | if (gt 0) ~(add 100) ~() | eq 105)
    | proves :runsOnlyTheChosenBranch
        |~~ The branch not chosen does not run. ~~|
        ~(5 | if true ~(1) ~(add "x") | eq 1)
    | proves :leavesTheSubjectByAnEmptyBranch
        |~~ The empty quote leaves the subject as it is. ~~|
        ~(-5 | if (gt 0) ~(add 100) ~() | eq -5)
    | proves :answersTheErrorOfAMissingField
        |~~ A condition that reads a field the record lacks answers its
            error.
        ~~|
        ~({} | if (get /flag) ~(1) ~(2) !| type | eq ::path/KeyNotInMapError)
    | proves :asksAMissingFieldByQuery
        |~~ A field that may be missing is asked by `query`. ~~|
        ~({} | if (query /flag | eq true) ~(1) ~(2) | eq 2)
    | proves :choosesForEachElement
        |~~ Under `map` the subject is each element. ~~|
        ~([1 -2 3] | map ~(if (gt 0) ~(it) ~(0)) | eq [1 0 3])
    | proves :condition/KindNotTakenError/refusesAQuote
        |~~ A condition given as a quote is code and no boolean. ~~|
        ~(5 | if ~(gt 0) ~(1) ~(2)
          !| get [~(type) /actualType]
          |  eq [::any/if/condition/KindNotTakenError ::quote])
    | proves :condition/KindNotTakenError/refusesNull
        |~~ A null condition is refused. ~~|
        ~({:flag null} | if (get /flag) ~(1) ~(2)
          !| get [~(type) /actualType]
          |  eq [::any/if/condition/KindNotTakenError ::null])
    | proves :condition/KindNotTakenError/refusesANumber
        |~~ A number condition is refused. ~~|
        ~(5 | if 1 ~("yes") ~("no")
          !| get [~(type) /actualType]
          |  eq [::any/if/condition/KindNotTakenError ::number])
    | proves :then/KindNotTakenError/refusesAString
        |~~ A string given as the then branch is refused. ~~|
        ~(5 | if true "yes" ~("no")
          !| get [~(type) /actualType]
          |  eq [::any/if/then/KindNotTakenError ::string])
    | proves :else/KindNotTakenError/refusesAString
        |~~ A string given as the else branch is refused. ~~|
        ~(5 | if true ~("yes") "no"
          !| get [~(type) /actualType]
          |  eq [::any/if/else/KindNotTakenError ::string])
    | proves :else/NotGivenError/refusesACallWithoutElse
        |~~ A call without the else branch is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~(5 | if (gt 0) ~(add 1) !| type | eq ::any/if/else/NotGivenError)
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesAThirdBranch
        |~~ A third branch is refused under the word. ~~|
        ~(5 | if true ~(1) ~(2) ~(3) !| type | eq ::any/if/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/coalesce
  |~~ Answers the first answer of the alternatives the call gives, run in
      order against the subject, that is neither null nor an error:
      `{:nickname null :name "alice"} | coalesce /nickname /name`
      answers `"alice"`. Every other value ends the walk, false, `0` and
      `""` among them, and when every alternative answers null or an
      error the call answers null. So `coalesce /key ~(fallback)`
      reads "the key where the map holds it, else the fallback", since
      `/key` answers an error where the map lacks the key. An
      alternative that is no code, a quote or a path, answers itself, and
      a call of such alternatives alone puts the subject first: `coalesce 30` reads
      "the subject, else 30". An error the steps before answered passes
      over the call, as over every step, so a field that may be missing
      is read inside an alternative, `coalesce /key 0`, not before
      it. The call takes one alternative at least.
      ::link~(::any/if | doc) ::link~(::any/cond | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :alternatives
        |~~ The alternatives, in their order, each code, a quote or a path,
            run against the subject, or a value that answers itself.
        ~~|
        ~(it)
    | holds :alternatives/EmptyError
        |~~ `coalesce` answers the first of its alternatives that answers
            a value, so it takes one at least,
            `coalesce /nickname ~("anonymous")`.
        ~~|
        ~(count | gt 0)
    | returns
        |~~ The first answer that is neither null nor an error, null when
            every alternative answers one.
        ~~|
        ::any
    | proves :passesOverNull
        |~~ A null answer passes to the next alternative. ~~|
        ~({:nickname null :name "alice"} | coalesce /nickname /name | eq "alice")
    | proves :answersTheFirstValue
        |~~ The first answer that is a value wins. ~~|
        ~({:nickname "ace" :name "alice"} | coalesce /nickname /name | eq "ace")
    | proves :keepsFalse
        |~~ False ends the walk as a value. ~~|
        ~({:on false} | coalesce /on ~(true) | not)
    | proves :answersNullWhenEveryAlternativeFails
        |~~ When every alternative answers null or an error, the call
            answers null.
        ~~|
        ~({} | coalesce /a /b | eq null)
    | proves :passesOverAFailure
        |~~ An error passes to the next alternative. ~~|
        ~({:a 2 :b 3} | coalesce /missing ~("fallback") | eq "fallback")
    | proves :fallsToTheLast
        |~~ The last alternative answers when the rest give nothing. ~~|
        ~({:nickname null :name null}
          | coalesce /nickname /name ~("anonymous") | eq "anonymous")
    | proves :takesAValueForAnAlternative
        |~~ A value stands for an alternative as its own answer. ~~|
        ~({:a 2} | coalesce /missing "fallback" | eq "fallback")
    | proves :defaultsAMissingField
        |~~ A field missing or null takes the default. ~~|
        ~([{:t 45} {:t null} {}] | map ~(query /t | coalesce 30) | eq [45 30 30])
    | proves :keepsAValue
        |~~ A value present stays. ~~|
        ~({:tags ["a"]} | get /tags | coalesce [] | eq ["a"])
    | proves :passesAnErrorBeforeIt
        |~~ An error the steps before answered passes over the call. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | get /missing | coalesce 0 !| type | eq ::path/KeyNotInMapError)
    | proves :alternatives/EmptyError/refusesNoAlternative
        |~~ A coalesce of no alternative is refused. ~~|
        ~({} | coalesce
          !| get [~(type) /actualValue]
          |  eq [::any/coalesce/alternatives/EmptyError ::any/coalesce[]])
    | proves :keepsZeroAndTheEmptyString
        |~~ Zero and the empty string end the walk. ~~|
        ~([0 ""] | map ~(coalesce 1) | eq [0 ""])
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/cond
  |~~ Answers the branch of the first predicate that holds of the subject,
      the clauses alternating predicate and branch, each a quote run
      against the subject in its turn:
      `85 | cond ~(gte 90) ~("A") ~(gte 80) ~("B") ~("F")` answers `"B"`,
      so a chain of nested `if` flattens into one call. A clause left over
      after the pairs is the default branch; without one, a walk that
      matches nothing answers null. Each predicate answers a boolean, and
      one that answers another kind is refused at its place. Unlike the
      condition of `if`, a predicate is code, a quote, and one computed at
      the call, `(gte 90)`, is refused at its index. The call takes one
      pair at least.
      ::link~(::any/if | doc) ::link~(::any/coalesce | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :clauses
        |~~ The clauses, each a quote: predicates and their branches in
            turn, a last one alone the default.
        ~~|
        ~(it) [#[::path ::quote]]
    | throws :clauses/ElementKindNotTakenError
        |~~ Each of the clauses is code, a quote, `~(gte 90)`: a predicate
            runs against the subject in its turn, so one computed at the
            call is refused at its index.
        ~~|
    | holds :clauses/NoBranchesError
        |~~ `cond` takes its clauses in pairs, a predicate and its branch,
            a last clause alone the default; a call without one pair, a
            lone default among them, has nothing to answer.
        ~~|
        ~(count | gte 2)
    | returns
        |~~ The answer of the branch chosen, null when no predicate holds
            and no default is given.
        ~~|
        ::any
    | throws :ConditionNotBooleanError
        |~~ Every predicate of `cond` answers a boolean; `:index` is the
            place in the args, from zero, of the predicate that answered
            another kind.
        ~~|
    | proves :picksTheFirstThatHolds
        |~~ Eighty-five is a B. ~~|
        ~(85 | cond ~(gte 90) ~("A") ~(gte 80) ~("B") ~(gte 70) ~("C") ~("F") | eq "B")
    | proves :readsTheSubject
        |~~ The predicates read the subject. ~~|
        ~({:role "admin"}
          | cond ~(get /role | eq "admin") ~("full") ~(get /role | eq "user") ~("limited")
          | eq "full")
    | proves :fallsToTheDefault
        |~~ A score no predicate holds of takes the default. ~~|
        ~(65 | cond ~(gte 90) ~("A") ~(gte 80) ~("B") ~(gte 70) ~("C") ~("F") | eq "F")
    | proves :answersNullWithoutADefault
        |~~ A walk that matches nothing without a default answers null. ~~|
        ~(5 | cond ~(gt 9) ~("big") | eq null)
    | proves :clauses/ElementKindNotTakenError/refusesAComputedPredicate
        |~~ A predicate computed at the call is refused at its index. ~~|
        ~(5 | cond (gt 3) ~("big")
          !| get [~(type) /index]
          |  eq [::any/cond/clauses/ElementKindNotTakenError 0])
    | proves :clauses/NoBranchesError/refusesNoPair
        |~~ A cond of no pair is refused. ~~|
        ~(5 | cond !| type | eq ::any/cond/clauses/NoBranchesError)
    | proves :clauses/NoBranchesError/refusesALoneDefault
        |~~ A lone default is no pair. ~~|
        ~(5 | cond ~("only") !| type | eq ::any/cond/clauses/NoBranchesError)
    | proves :ConditionNotBooleanError/refusesANumberAnswer
        |~~ A predicate that answers a number is refused at its place. ~~|
        ~(5 | cond ~(1) ~("yes")
          !| get [~(type) /index /actualType]
          |  eq [::any/cond/ConditionNotBooleanError 0 ::number])
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/apply
  |~~ Answers the code the call gives run against the subject,
      `5 | apply ~(add 1)` answering `6`. The code is a quote, a path or
      a function, or a name that holds one, `apply double`, and a
      function takes its arguments as a vector, as a call holds its
      objects, `5 | apply f [3]`. Its steps run in order, a
      leading `!|` routing the first through the fail track, so a
      pipeline suffix a `:trail` records runs again, as held code runs. Code held
      as the subject runs on itself by `apply it`, and text runs once
      `parse` reads it, `"10 | add 3" | parse | apply it`. The code runs
      under the fork rule: its declarations stay inside it, and only its
      value comes out. A step written without its quote runs at the
      call, so `apply (add 1)` is refused, as is the error a step
      answered there. The call takes one code; code of several steps is
      one quote.
      ::link~(::quote | doc) ::link~(::fn | doc) ::link~(::string/parse | doc)
      ::link~(::qlang/code | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :code
        |~~ The code the call runs, a quote, a path or a function, or a
            name that holds one.
        ~~|
        /0 #[::fn ::path ::quote]
    | throws :code/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The code is a quote, `apply ~(add 1)`, a path, `apply /a`, or
            a function: a step written without its quote runs at the call,
            and its answer, an error among them, is no code.
        ~~|
    | throws :code/NotGivenError
        |~~ `apply` runs the code the call gives, `apply ~(add 1)`; code
            held as the subject runs on itself by `apply it`.
        ~~|
    | takes :args
        |~~ The arguments of a function, a vector, as a call holds its
            objects.
        ~~|
        ~(query /1 []) ::vec
    | throws :args/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The arguments of a function are a vector, `apply f [3]`, as a
            call holds its objects.
        ~~|
    | throws :args/NotTakenError
        |~~ A quote and a path take no arguments: steps run in order
            inside one quote, `apply ~(add 1 | mul 2)`, and arguments go
            to a function, `apply f [3]`.
        ~~|
    | returns |~~ The answer of the code run against the subject. ~~| ::any
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `apply` takes the code and the arguments of a function, one
            vector, `apply f [3]`.
        ~~|
    | proves :runsTheCode
        |~~ The code runs against the subject. ~~|
        ~(5 | apply ~(add 1) | eq 6)
    | proves :runsANameThatHoldsCode
        |~~ A name that holds a quote runs as the quote. ~~|
        ~(:double ~(mul 2) | 5 | apply double | eq 10)
    | proves :runsStepsInOrder
        |~~ Several steps run in order. ~~|
        ~([[1 2] [3 4]] | apply ~(flat | sort) | eq [1 2 3 4])
    | proves :runsParsedText
        |~~ Text parsed into a quote runs. ~~|
        ~("10 | add 3" | parse | apply it | eq 13)
    | proves :keepsTheDeclarationsInside
        |~~ The declarations of the code stay inside it. ~~|
        ~(5 | apply ~(:x 1 | x) | :y it | env | has :x | not)
    | proves :code/KindNotTakenError/refusesANumber
        |~~ A number computed at the call is refused. ~~|
        ~(42 | apply 1 !| get [~(type) /actualType] | eq [::any/apply/code/KindNotTakenError ::number])
    | proves :code/KindNotTakenError/refusesTheErrorOfAStep
        |~~ The error a step answered at the call is refused as no code. ~~|
        ~(5 | apply (get /nope)
          !| get [~(type) /actualType]
          |  eq [::any/apply/code/KindNotTakenError ::path/SubjectHasNoPartsError])
    | proves :code/NotGivenError/refusesACallWithNoCode
        |~~ A call with no code is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~(~(add 1) | apply !| type | eq ::any/apply/code/NotGivenError)
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesAThirdObject
        |~~ A call with an object after the arguments is refused under the
            word.
        ~~|
        ~(5 | apply ~(add 1) [] [] !| type | eq ::any/apply/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | proves :args/NotTakenError/refusesTheArgumentsOfAQuote
        |~~ A second quote is no argument of the first. ~~|
        ~(5 | apply ~(add 1) ~(mul 2) !| type | eq ::any/apply/args/NotTakenError)
    | proves :args/KindNotTakenError/refusesANumber
        |~~ A number is no vector of arguments. ~~|
        ~(5 | apply ~(add 1) 3 !| type | eq ::any/apply/args/KindNotTakenError)
    | proves :runsAFunctionOnItsArguments
        |~~ A function runs on the subject with its arguments. ~~|
        ~(5 | apply (::fn | takes :by |~~ How much. ~~| /0 | impl ~(add by)) [3] | eq 8)
    | proves :passesAValueByALeadingFailStep
        |~~ A leading `!|` passes a value on. ~~|
        ~(5 | apply ~(!| 0) | eq 5)
    | proves :runsARecordedSuffixAgain
        |~~ The steps a trail skipped run again on a value. ~~|
        ~(1 | add "x" | mul 2 | add 3 !| (:rest (get /trail/0/skipped) | 5 | apply rest) | eq 13)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/env
  |~~ Answers what the reader declared before the step, whatever the
      subject, the cells of a session before it among them: a map from
      each name to the record of its binding, `::binding` with its name,
      its docs, its value, its source and its origin. `:limit 10 | env`
      answers the record of `:limit`, and `env | get /limit` hands it to the
      next step. A name with a path is held by its address. Inside a
      fork it holds the names before the fork and the fork's own, and
      the fork's stay inside it. What the language and the host declared
      stands apart, so at the start of a query it answers `{}`: `kinds`
      lists their kinds, and `manifest` what lies under a noun. The call
      takes no name.
      ::link~(::any/binding | doc) ::link~(::any/kinds | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns
        |~~ The records of what the reader declared, by name. ~~|
        ::map
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `env` answers every name the reader declared and takes none:
            one record is read by its key, `env | get /limit`.
        ~~|
    | proves :holdsADeclaredName
        |~~ A name declared before the step is held. ~~|
        ~(:limit 10 | env | has :limit)
    | proves :answersWhateverTheSubject
        |~~ The subject leaves the answer as it is. ~~|
        ~(:limit 10 | [1 2] | env | has :limit)
    | proves :holdsTheRecord
        |~~ Each name holds the record its declaration wrote. ~~|
        ~(:limit 10 | env | get /limit | get /value | eq 10)
    | proves :holdsANameWithAPath
        |~~ A name with a path is held by its address. ~~|
        ~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~| | :Box/area ~(1) | env | keys | eq #[:Box :Box/area])
    | proves :holdsTheNamesOfAFork
        |~~ Inside a fork the names before it and its own are held. ~~|
        ~(:a 1 | (:b 2 | env | keys) | eq #[:a :b])
    | proves :keepsTheNamesOfAForkInside
        |~~ The names of a fork stay inside it. ~~|
        ~(:a 1 | (:b 2) | env | keys | eq #[:a])
    | proves :isEmptyAtTheStart
        |~~ Nothing is declared at the start. ~~|
        ~(env | eq {})
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesAName
        |~~ A name given to `env` is refused under the word. ~~|
        ~(:limit 10 | env :limit !| type | eq ::any/env/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/kinds
  |~~ Answers the set of the kinds the scope reads, each by its tag name,
      whatever the subject: those the language and the host declared,
      the topic of the language among them, and those the reader
      declares. A query asks which kinds there are before it asks one of
      them for its page, `::set | doc`, or for what lies under it,
      `::set | manifest`. A concept of the topic is no kind:
      `::qlang/values` is a page `doc` reads. The call takes no kind.
      ::link~(::any/env | doc) ::link~(::qlang | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The tag names of the kinds the scope reads. ~~| ::set
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `kinds` answers every kind and takes none: whether a kind is
            there is `kinds | has ::set`.
        ~~|
    | proves :holdsTheKindsOfTheLanguage
        |~~ The kinds of the language are read. ~~|
        ~(kinds | has ::vec)
    | proves :answersWhateverTheSubject
        |~~ The subject leaves the answer as it is. ~~|
        ~("text" | kinds | has ::vec)
    | proves :holdsTheTopic
        |~~ The topic of the language is a label too. ~~|
        ~(kinds | has ::qlang)
    | proves :holdsAReadersKind
        |~~ A kind the reader declares is read. ~~|
        ~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~| | kinds | has ::Box)
    | proves :holdsNoConcept
        |~~ A concept of the topic is no kind. ~~|
        ~(kinds | has ::qlang/values | not)
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesAKind
        |~~ A kind given to `kinds` is refused under the word. ~~|
        ~(kinds ::set !| type | eq ::any/kinds/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/binding
  |~~ Answers the record of a name, what its declaration wrote into its
      scope: `::binding` with its `:name`, its `:docs`, its `:value`, its
      `:source` and its `:origin`, the source it was read from, so where a
      name was declared is read as data,
      `::vec/filter | binding | get /origin` answering `:qlang/vec`. A
      word by its address reads the record its source declared, a name of
      the scope the record its declaration wrote, and any other value the
      record of its kind. The anchor, a keyword after it, reads the record
      of the member the subject holds under it, an input of a word or the
      word a value calls. `doc` reads the page of the record, and
      `source` every declaration written under the name's address, the
      record's `:source` holding the step of the declaration that wrote
      it. A name nothing declares is refused, with
      the addresses where a word of that name lives.
      ::link~(::any/doc | doc) ::link~(::any/source | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :anchor
        |~~ The member read under the name, a keyword, null when the call
            gives none.
        ~~|
        ~(query /0) #[::keyword ::null]
    | throws :anchor/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The anchor is a keyword or null, the last name of a name under
            the subject, `::vec | binding :sort`.
        ~~|
    | returns |~~ The record of the name. ~~| ::binding
    | throws :UnknownNameError
        |~~ `binding` found no declaration of the name it read: a keyword
            that names no binding of its scope, the last name of a word
            declared under a kind among them, or a tag name that names
            nothing. `:name` holds the name, and `:addresses` the
            addresses of the pages of that name, one for each kind that
            answers it, so the word is read by one of them,
            `::vec/filter | binding`. `source`, `doc`, `explain`, `laws`
            and `runLaws` read a name as `binding` does, and each is
            refused under a tag of its own, so the tag says which of them
            read it. `:nearest` holds the names near the one it missed,
            the tags as deep as a tag it read, and for a member the subject
            lacks the members it holds and the words its walk reaches.
        ~~|
    | proves :answersABinding
        |~~ The record is a `::binding`. ~~|
        ~(::vec/filter | binding | type | eq ::binding)
    | proves :readsTheOriginOfAVerb
        |~~ A word's record names the source that declared it. ~~|
        ~(::vec/filter | binding | get /origin | eq :qlang/vec)
    | proves :readsTheValueOfAName
        |~~ A name's record holds its value. ~~|
        ~(:x 1 | ::x | binding | get /value | eq 1)
    | proves :readsTheKindOfAValue
        |~~ A number reads the record of `::number`. ~~|
        ~(5 | binding | get /name | eq :number)
    | proves :readsAnInputByAnAnchor
        |~~ An anchor reads the record of an input of a word. ~~|
        ~(::vec/take | binding :count | get /name | eq :vec/take/count)
    | proves :readsTheWordAValueCalls
        |~~ An anchor on a value reads the record of the word it calls. ~~|
        ~([1] | binding :count | get /name | eq :vec/count)
    | proves :holdsThePageDocReads
        |~~ The record holds the page `doc` reads. ~~|
        ~(:x |~~ The x. ~~| 1 | ::x | binding | get /docs | first | eq (::x | doc))
    | proves :anchor/KindNotTakenError/refusesANumber
        |~~ An anchor that is a number is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~(::vec/take | binding 1 !| type | eq ::any/binding/anchor/KindNotTakenError)
    | proves :UnknownNameError/refusesANameNothingDeclares
        |~~ A name nothing declares is refused. ~~|
        ~(::nonexistent-binding | binding
          !| get [~(type) /name]
          |  eq [::any/binding/UnknownNameError ::nonexistent-binding])
    | proves :UnknownNameError/namesWhereTheWordLives
        |~~ A word's name is refused with the addresses where the word
            lives.
        ~~|
        ~(::filter | binding !| get /addresses | has ::vec/filter)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/source
  |~~ Answers the code written under a name, one quote of its
      declarations in the order they were written, which `parse` prints
      as its text, a declaration a line: the declaration that binds the
      name in its scope, the chain of a word's function with its inputs,
      its answer, its refusals and its laws, and every declaration under
      its address. `::Tag | source` reads what was written
      under the tag, a refusal its own declaration, and any other value
      the declaration of its kind. A binding no step declared, a value a
      host bound, answers null. Given a name, it reads the name the
      subject holds under it: for a tag name the name under its address,
      an input of a word among them, and for any other subject
      the word the name calls after it. A name nothing declares is
      refused, with the addresses where a word of that name lives.
      ::link~(::any/doc | doc) ::link~(::any/binding | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :name
        |~~ The name read under the subject, a keyword, null when the call
            gives none.
        ~~|
        ~(query /0) #[::keyword ::null]
    | throws :name/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The name is a keyword or null, the last name of a name under
            the subject, `::vec | source :sort`.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ The code written under the name, null for a value a host
            bound.
        ~~|
        #[::quote ::null]
    | throws :UnknownNameError
        |~~ `source` found no declaration of the name it read, as
            `binding` finds none.
            ::link~(::any/binding/UnknownNameError | doc)
        ~~|
    | proves :readsEveryDeclarationUnderAName
        |~~ The source of a word is its declaration, the chain that holds
            its inputs, its refusals and its laws.
        ~~|
        ~(::vec/take | source | map /name | eq [:vec/take])
    | proves :readsInTheOrderWritten
        |~~ The declarations under a name read in the order they were
            written.
        ~~|
        ~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~| | :Box/a ~(get /0) | :Box/b ~(get /1) | :Box/note |~~ A note. ~~|
          | ::Box | source | map /name
          | eq [:Box :Box/a :Box/b :Box/note])
    | proves :printsTheDeclaration
        |~~ The source of a word prints as its declaration. ~~|
        ~(::vec/filter | source | parse | contains ":vec/filter")
    | proves :printsADeclarationALine
        |~~ The source of a word prints a declaration a line. ~~|
        ~(::number/round | source | parse | lines | first | eq ":number/round |~~ Answers the subject rounded to the digits after the point the call")
    | proves :printsARefusalInTheChainOfItsKind
        |~~ A refusal a kind pages prints in the source of the kind, the
            chain that declares it.
        ~~|
        ~(::number | source | parse | contains "throws :NotFiniteError")
    | proves :readsTheKindOfAValue
        |~~ A string reads the source of `::string`. ~~|
        ~("text" | source | eq (::string | source))
    | proves :readsAWordByAnAnchor
        |~~ A name after a kind reads the source of its word. ~~|
        ~(::vec | source :take | eq (::vec/take | source))
    | proves :readsTheWordAValueCalls
        |~~ A name on a value reads the source of the word it calls. ~~|
        ~([1] | source :count | eq (::vec/count | source))
    | proves :name/KindNotTakenError/refusesANumber
        |~~ A name that is a number is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~(::vec/take | source 1 !| type | eq ::any/source/name/KindNotTakenError)
    | proves :UnknownNameError/refusesANameNothingDeclares
        |~~ A name nothing declares is refused. ~~|
        ~(::nonexistent-binding | source
          !| get [~(type) /name]
          |  eq [::any/source/UnknownNameError ::nonexistent-binding])
    | proves :UnknownNameError/namesWhereTheWordLives
        |~~ A word's name is refused with the addresses where the word
            lives.
        ~~|
        ~(::filter | source !| get /addresses | has ::vec/filter)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/doc
  |~~ Answers the page of a name, the passage from an address into its
      description: a name is read by its tag, `::vec/filter | doc` the
      page of a word, `::limit | doc` the page of the declaration that
      binds `limit` in the scope, null for a declaration without one. A
      page answers its text by `content`, its links by `links` and its
      snippets by `snippets`, and the laws of the name stand under it,
      `::vec/take | laws`. Any other value reads the page of its kind, a
      keyword among them, `:limit | doc` the page of `::keyword`, and the
      `:kind` field of a map names nothing. The anchor, a keyword
      after `doc`, reads the page of the member the subject holds under
      it, the word a value calls or an input of a word; a vector of
      anchors walks a path, each read within the page the one before
      reached, and a set of anchors reads a page at each. A name nothing
      declares is refused, with the addresses where a word of that name
      lives, as is an anchor no member answers.
      ::link~(::any/laws | doc) ::link~(::doc | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :anchor
        |~~ The anchor within the page: a keyword, a vector of them that
            walks a path or a set of them, null when the call gives none.
        ~~|
        ~(query /0) #[::keyword ::null ::set ::vec]
    | throws :anchor/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The anchor is a keyword, a vector, a set or null, the last
            names of names under the subject, `::qlang | doc :pipeline`.
        ~~|
    | throws :anchor/ElementNotKeywordError
        |~~ An anchor of the path `doc` reads is no keyword: each step of
            the path names a member by a keyword, `doc [:take :count]`.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ The page of the name, null for a declaration without one, a
            vector of pages for a set of anchors.
        ~~|
        #[::doc ::null ::vec]
    | throws :UnknownNameError
        |~~ `doc` found no declaration of the name it read, as `binding`
            finds none, or no member of the page before it under an anchor
            of its path.
            ::link~(::any/binding/UnknownNameError | doc)
        ~~|
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `doc` reads one anchor: a path of anchors is a vector,
            `doc [:take :count]`, and the pages of several a set,
            `doc #[:take :drop]`.
        ~~|
    | proves :readsThePageOfAVerb
        |~~ The page of `filter` speaks of its predicate. ~~|
        ~(::vec/filter | doc | content | contains "predicate")
    | proves :answersNullForADeclarationWithoutAPage
        |~~ A declaration without a page answers null. ~~|
        ~(:x 1 | ::x | doc | eq null)
    | proves :readsThePageOfARefusal
        |~~ A refusal's page is a doc. ~~|
        ~(::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError | doc | type | eq ::doc)
    | proves :readsThePageOfAKind
        |~~ A number reads the page of `::number`. ~~|
        ~(5 | doc | eq (::number | doc))
    | proves :readsAKeywordByItsKind
        |~~ A keyword is a value and names nothing: it reads the page of
            `::keyword`, and the declaration it spells is read by its tag.
        ~~|
        ~(:limit |~~ The cap. ~~| 5
          | get [~(:limit | doc) ~(::limit | doc | content)]
          | eq (get [~(::keyword | doc) " The cap. "]))
    | proves :readsNoKindFromAField
        |~~ A map reads the page of maps, whatever its `:kind` says. ~~|
        ~({:kind ::set} | doc | eq (::map | doc))
    | proves :readsAMemberByAnAnchor
        |~~ An anchor reads the page of a member. ~~|
        ~(::map | doc :minus | eq (::map/minus | doc))
    | proves :readsTheWordAValueCalls
        |~~ An anchor on a value reads the page of the word it calls. ~~|
        ~([1 2] | doc :count | eq (::vec/count | doc))
    | proves :readsAnInputOfAWord
        |~~ An anchor reads the page of an input of a word. ~~|
        ~(::number/add | doc :addend | eq (::number/add/addend | doc))
    | proves :walksAPath
        |~~ A vector of anchors walks a path. ~~|
        ~(::vec | doc [:take] | eq (::vec/take | doc))
    | proves :readsAPageAtEachAnchor
        |~~ A set of anchors reads a page at each. ~~|
        ~(::qlang | doc #[:pipeline :errors] | count | eq 2)
    | proves :anchor/KindNotTakenError/refusesANumber
        |~~ An anchor that is a number is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~(::vec | doc 5 !| type | eq ::any/doc/anchor/KindNotTakenError)
    | proves :UnknownNameError/refusesANameNothingDeclares
        |~~ A name nothing declares is refused. ~~|
        ~(::nonexistent-binding | doc
          !| get [~(type) /name]
          |  eq [::any/doc/UnknownNameError ::nonexistent-binding])
    | proves :UnknownNameError/namesWhereTheWordLives
        |~~ A word's name is refused with the addresses where the word
            lives.
        ~~|
        ~(::filter | doc !| get /addresses | has ::vec/filter)
    | proves :UnknownNameError/namesWhereAnotherWordLives
        |~~ Another word's name is refused with its addresses. ~~|
        ~(::reduce | doc !| get /addresses | has ::vec/reduce)
    | proves :UnknownNameError/refusesAnAnchorNoMemberAnswers
        |~~ An anchor no member answers is refused with the name it read.
        ~~|
        ~(::vec/take | doc :nope !| get [~(type) /name] | eq [::any/doc/UnknownNameError :nope])
    | proves :UnknownNameError/namesTheMembersNear
        |~~ A member a page lacks names the members near it. ~~|
        ~(::qlang | doc :pipelin !| get /nearest | eq [:pipeline])
    | proves :anchor/ElementNotKeywordError/refusesANumberInThePath
        |~~ A number in the path is refused at its index. ~~|
        ~(::vec | doc [:take 1] !| get [~(type) /index] | eq [::any/doc/anchor/ElementNotKeywordError 1])
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesTwoAnchors
        |~~ Two anchors given apart are refused under the word. ~~|
        ~(::vec | doc :take :count !| type | eq ::any/doc/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/explain
  |~~ Answers the explanation of the subject,
      `::explanation{:page … :value …}`: the page `doc` reads for it, then
      the value itself, so it prints as the page above what it explains. A
      name reads its own page, a value the page of its kind, and an error
      `!|` opened the page of the refusal it carries, which says what
      failed and how the call is repaired. A name nothing declares is
      refused as `binding` refuses it. The call takes no anchor: the page
      of a member is read by `doc`.
      ::link~(::any/doc | doc) ::link~(::explanation | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The page above the value it explains. ~~| ::explanation
    | throws :UnknownNameError
        |~~ `explain` found no declaration of the page of its subject, as
            `binding` finds none.
            ::link~(::any/binding/UnknownNameError | doc)
        ~~|
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `explain` explains its subject and takes no anchor: the page
            of a member is read by `doc`, `[1 2] | doc :count`, and the
            member is explained by its address, `::vec/count | explain`.
        ~~|
    | proves :explainsAValueByItsKind
        |~~ A number is explained by the page of its kind. ~~|
        ~(42 | explain | page | eq (::number | doc))
    | proves :holdsThePageAndTheValue
        |~~ The explanation holds the page `doc` reads, then the value it
            explains.
        ~~|
        ~(42 | explain | get [~(page | type) ~(value)] | eq [::doc 42])
    | proves :explainsAWordByItsPage
        |~~ A word is explained by its page. ~~|
        ~(::number/add | explain | page | content | contains "plus the number")
    | proves :explainsAnErrorByItsSite
        |~~ An error is explained by the page of the refusal it carries. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1 !| explain | page | content | contains "adds to a number")
    | proves :UnknownNameError/refusesANameNothingDeclares
        |~~ A name nothing declares is refused. ~~|
        ~(::nonexistent-binding | explain
          !| get [~(type) /name]
          |  eq [::any/explain/UnknownNameError ::nonexistent-binding])
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesAnAnchor
        |~~ An anchor given to `explain` is refused under the word. ~~|
        ~([1 2] | explain :count !| type | eq ::any/explain/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/manifest
  |~~ Answers the set of the names declared one name under the name the
      subject reads, as `doc` reads it, each by its address:
      `::number | manifest` the words and the refusals of numbers,
      `::vec/take | manifest` the inputs and the answer of `take`,
      `::qlang | manifest` the chapters of the language, and a value the
      names under the kind it shows, `5 | manifest` those of numbers. A
      name nothing declares is refused under the word,
      `::any/manifest/UnknownNameError`. The laws of a
      name are no part of the answer, `laws` reads them; the kinds of the
      scope `kinds` lists, and what the reader declared `env`; and what
      one name holds the axes read, `::vec/count | source`, `| doc`. The
      call takes no value, so `manifest ::vec` is refused.
      ::snippet~(::number | manifest) ::link~(::any/laws | doc)
      ::link~(::any/kinds | doc) ::link~(::any/env | doc)
      ::link~(::any/source | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns
        |~~ The addresses of the names declared one name under the name. ~~|
        ::set
    | throws :UnknownNameError
        |~~ `manifest` found no declaration of the name it read, as
            `binding` finds none.
            ::link~(::any/binding/UnknownNameError | doc)
        ~~|
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `manifest` reads the name of the subject and takes no value:
            the manifest of vectors is `::vec | manifest`.
        ~~|
    | proves :holdsAVerbOfTheNoun
        |~~ The manifest of numbers holds `add`. ~~|
        ~(::number | manifest | has ::number/add)
    | proves :holdsAChapter
        |~~ The manifest of the first page holds its chapter on values. ~~|
        ~(::qlang | manifest | has ::qlang/values)
    | proves :holdsTheInputsOfAWord
        |~~ The manifest of a word holds its inputs. ~~|
        ~(::vec/take | manifest | has ::vec/take/count)
    | proves :holdsTheNamesOfAReader
        |~~ The manifest of a reader's noun holds the names declared under
            it.
        ~~|
        ~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~| | :Box/area ~(1) | ::Box | manifest | eq #[::Box/area])
    | proves :leavesTheLawsToLaws
        |~~ The laws of a name are no part of its manifest. ~~|
        ~(::number/add | get [~(manifest | filter ~(binding | get /value | is ::law) | count) ~(laws | count | gt 0)] | eq [0 true])
    | proves :readsTheKindOfAValue
        |~~ A value reads the names under the kind it shows. ~~|
        ~(get [5 :number] | map ~manifest | eq (get [~(::number | manifest) ~(::keyword | manifest)]))
    | proves :readsPastATagOfIdentity
        |~~ A value under a tag nothing declares reads the kind beneath it. ~~|
        ~(::Box{:a 1} | manifest | eq (::map | manifest))
    | proves :UnknownNameError/refusesANameNothingDeclares
        |~~ A name nothing declares is refused. ~~|
        ~(::Nothing | manifest
          !| get [~(type) /name]
          |  eq [::any/manifest/UnknownNameError ::Nothing])
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesANounGivenAsAValue
        |~~ A noun given as a value is refused; it is the subject. ~~|
        ~(::qlang | manifest ::vec !| type | eq ::any/manifest/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/laws
  |~~ Answers the laws declared under the name the subject reads, as
      `doc` reads it, in the order of their declarations: a map from
      each law's name, which says the claim it proves, to the pair of its
      page, the sentence of the claim, and its query, which answers
      true. A value reads the laws of its kind, and a name with no laws
      answers `{}`. `runLaws` runs them, and `tutorial` pairs them with
      the page. A name nothing declares is refused as `binding` refuses
      it. The call takes no anchor: the laws of a member are read by its
      address.
      ::link~(::any/runLaws | doc) ::link~(::any/tutorial | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns
        |~~ The name of each law and the pair of its page and its query. ~~|
        ::map
    | throws :UnknownNameError
        |~~ `laws` found no declaration of the name it read, as `binding`
            finds none.
            ::link~(::any/binding/UnknownNameError | doc)
        ~~|
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `laws` reads the laws of its subject and takes no anchor: the
            laws of a member are read by its address, `::vec/take | laws`.
        ~~|
    | proves :answersEachLawByItsName
        |~~ A law declared under a name is answered under its own name. ~~|
        ~(:k 5 | :k/answersFive |~~ k answers five. ~~| ::law~(k | eq 5)
          | ::k | laws | keys | eq #[:answersFive])
    | proves :pairsThePageWithTheQuery
        |~~ Each law is the pair of its page and its query. ~~|
        ~(:k 5 | :k/answersFive |~~ k answers five. ~~| ::law~(k | eq 5)
          | ::k | laws | get /answersFive | get [~(get /0 | content) /1]
          | eq [" k answers five. " ~(k | eq 5)])
    | proves :readsTheLawsOfTheKindOfAValue
        |~~ A value reads the laws of its kind. ~~|
        ~(5 | laws | eq (::number | laws))
    | proves :answersNoLawsAsAnEmptyMap
        |~~ A name with no laws answers an empty map. ~~|
        ~(:k 5 | ::k | laws | eq {})
    | proves :UnknownNameError/refusesANameNothingDeclares
        |~~ A name nothing declares is refused. ~~|
        ~(::nonexistent-binding | laws
          !| get [~(type) /name]
          |  eq [::any/laws/UnknownNameError ::nonexistent-binding])
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesAnAnchor
        |~~ An anchor given to `laws` is refused under the word. ~~|
        ~(::vec | laws :take !| type | eq ::any/laws/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/runLaws
  |~~ Answers the results of the laws under the name the subject reads,
      as `doc` reads it, a word by its address among them, one
      `{:law :says :actual :error :ok}` per law in their order: `:law`
      its address, `:says` its page, the claim it proves, `:actual` what
      its query answered, null where it answered an error, which `:error`
      holds as the error at rest, and `:ok` whether the answer is true,
      so a law that answers false or an error fails. A value runs the
      laws of its kind, and a name of the scope the laws declared under
      it. The anchor, a keyword after it, runs the laws of the member the
      name holds under it, a concept of the language among them. A name
      nothing declares is refused under the word,
      `::any/runLaws/UnknownNameError`.
      ::link~(::any/laws | doc) ::link~(::any/tutorial | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :anchor
        |~~ The member whose laws run, a keyword, null when the call gives
            none.
        ~~|
        ~(query /0) #[::keyword ::null]
    | throws :anchor/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The anchor is a keyword or null, the last name of a name under
            the subject, `::vec | runLaws :sort`.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ A map for each law: the law, its claim, what it answered and
            whether it held.
        ~~|
        ::vec
    | throws :UnknownNameError
        |~~ `runLaws` found no declaration of the name it read, as
            `binding` finds none.
            ::link~(::any/binding/UnknownNameError | doc)
        ~~|
    | proves :runsTheLawsOfAVerb
        |~~ The first law of `add` holds. ~~|
        ~(::number/add | runLaws | first | get /ok)
    | proves :holdsEveryLawOfAdd
        |~~ Every law of `add` holds. ~~|
        ~(::number/add | runLaws | map /ok | distinct | eq #[true])
    | proves :failsALawThatAnswersFalse
        |~~ A law that answers false fails. ~~|
        ~(:k 5 | :k/wrong |~~ One is two. ~~| ::law~(1 | eq 2)
          | ::k | runLaws | first | get /ok | not)
    | proves :runsTheLawsOfTheKindOfAValue
        |~~ A value runs the laws of its kind, as `doc` reads its page. ~~|
        ~(5 | runLaws | count | eq (::number | runLaws | count))
    | proves :runsTheLawsUnderTheName
        |~~ The laws under the name a keyword reads run. ~~|
        ~(:limit |~~ A limit. ~~| 10 | :limit/holds |~~ It holds. ~~| ::law~(true)
          | ::limit | runLaws | map /ok | eq [true])
    | proves :runsTheLawsUnderAnAnchor
        |~~ An anchor runs the laws of the name under the one read. ~~|
        ~(::qlang | runLaws :values | every /ok)
    | proves :anchor/KindNotTakenError/refusesANumber
        |~~ An anchor that is a number is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~(::qlang | runLaws 1 !| type | eq ::any/runLaws/anchor/KindNotTakenError)
    | proves :UnknownNameError/refusesANameNothingDeclares
        |~~ A name nothing declares is refused. ~~|
        ~(::nonexistent-binding | runLaws
          !| get [~(type) /name]
          |  eq [::any/runLaws/UnknownNameError ::nonexistent-binding])
    | proves :UnknownNameError/refusesATagNothingDeclares
        |~~ A tag nothing declares is refused. ~~|
        ~(::Nonexistent | runLaws
          !| get [~(type) /name]
          |  eq [::any/runLaws/UnknownNameError ::Nonexistent])
    | proves :UnknownNameError/namesWhereTheWordLives
        |~~ A word's name is refused with the addresses where the word
            lives.
        ~~|
        ~(::filter | runLaws !| get /addresses | has ::vec/filter)
    | proves :failsALawThatAnswersAFailure
        |~~ A law that answers an error fails, and `:error` holds it at
            rest.
        ~~|
        ~(:k 5 | :k/bad |~~ b ~~| ::law~(add "x") | ::k | runLaws | first
          | get [/ok /actual ~(get /error | type)] | eq [false null ::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError])
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/tutorial
  |~~ Answers the page of the name the subject reads and its laws, the
      pair a reader learns the name from: the prose that says what it
      is, and the laws that show it at work, each the pair of its claim
      and its query, `::number | tutorial`. A value reads the page and
      the laws of its kind. A name nothing declares is refused as `doc`
      refuses it, under the tag of `doc`. The call takes no anchor: a
      member is read by its address, `::vec/take | tutorial`.
      ::link~(::any/doc | doc) ::link~(::any/laws | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The page and the laws. ~~| ::vec
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `tutorial` reads the name of its subject and takes no anchor:
            a member is read by its address, `::vec/take | tutorial`.
        ~~|
    | proves :pairsThePageWithTheLaws
        |~~ A tutorial is the page of a name and its laws. ~~|
        ~(::number | tutorial | get [~(get /0 | type) ~(get /1 | type)] | eq [::doc ::map])
    | proves :readsTheKindOfAValue
        |~~ A value reads the page and the laws of its kind. ~~|
        ~(5 | tutorial | eq (::number | tutorial))
    | proves :refusesANameNothingDeclares
        |~~ A name nothing declares is refused as `doc` refuses it. ~~|
        ~(::nothere | tutorial !| type | eq ::any/doc/UnknownNameError)
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesAnAnchor
        |~~ An anchor given to `tutorial` is refused under the word. ~~|
        ~(::vec | tutorial :take !| type | eq ::any/tutorial/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | impl ~(:subject it | doc | get [~(it) ~(subject | laws)])

:any/tag
  |~~ Answers the subject under the name the call gives, a tag name:
      `5 | tag ::Box` answers `::Box#5`, a value minted at run time as
      the literal `::Box#5` mints it where it is written, its type the
      tag and its payload the subject. Given no name, it reads the pair
      `get [~(type) ~(payload)]` answers, `[::Box 5] | tag`, so a value split tags
      back into itself. A tag of a kind mints through the kind:
      `5 | tag ::number` answers `5`, a payload the kind refuses is
      refused, `"5" | tag ::number`, and a kind the reader declares
      holds its invariants. A tag over a tagged value nests it;
      replacing the tag goes through the payload,
      `payload | tag ::Crate`. A tag over an error at rest names its
      site, `[::Late !{:by 3}] | tag` answering `::Late!{:by 3}`, and
      `raise` raises it under that tag.
      The call takes one name; a keyword, `:box`, names a field and no
      kind.
      ::link~(::any/type | doc) ::link~(::tagged/payload | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :name
        |~~ The tag laid over the subject, null when the call gives none
            and the subject is a pair.
        ~~|
        ~(query /0) #[::null ::tag]
    | throws :name/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The name is a tag name, `::Box`; a keyword, `:box`, names a
            field and no kind.
        ~~|
    | returns |~~ The subject under the tag, read by its kind. ~~| ::any
    | throws :PairTagNotTagError
        |~~ The tag of the pair `tag` reads is a tag name,
            `[::Box 5] | tag`; a keyword, `:box`, names a field and no
            kind.
        ~~|
    | throws :SubjectNotPairError
        |~~ `tag` alone reads a pair, `[::Box value] | tag`, the tag and
            the value it goes over; a subject of another shape is refused,
            a vector of another length among them.
        ~~|
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `tag` lays one tag: two tags nest one call after the other,
            `tag ::Box | tag ::Crate`, and a pair whose value comes first
            turns before it is read, `get [/1 /0] | tag`.
        ~~|
    | proves :standsOverTheValue
        |~~ The payload of a tagged number is the number. ~~|
        ~(42 | tag ::Box | payload | eq 42)
    | proves :namesTheKind
        |~~ The tag is the type of the value. ~~|
        ~([1 2 3] | tag ::Triple | type | eq ::Triple)
    | proves :readsAPair
        |~~ Given no name, `tag` reads a pair. ~~|
        ~([::Box 5] | tag | eq ::Box#5)
    | proves :tagsASplitBack
        |~~ A split value tags back into itself. ~~|
        ~(:Box |~~ A box. ~~| | ::Box[1 2 3] | get [~(type) ~(payload)] | tag | eq ::Box[1 2 3])
    | proves :mintsThroughTheKind
        |~~ A tag of a kind of the core reads the payload as the kind. ~~|
        ~(5 | tag ::number | eq 5)
    | proves :refusesAPayloadTheKindRefuses
        |~~ A payload the kind refuses is refused by the kind. ~~|
        ~("5" | tag ::number !| type | eq ::number/PayloadKindNotTakenError)
    | proves :holdsTheInvariantsOfAReadersKind
        |~~ A kind the reader declares holds its invariants. ~~|
        ~(:Pos |~~ A positive number. ~~| (::kind | holds :NotPositiveError |~~ A number above zero. ~~| ~(gt 0)) | -3 | tag ::Pos !| type | eq ::Pos/NotPositiveError)
    | proves :nestsATaggedValue
        |~~ A tag over a tagged value nests it. ~~|
        ~(:Inner |~~ An inner kind. ~~| | :Outer |~~ An outer kind. ~~|
          | ::Inner[1] | tag ::Outer | payload | type | eq ::Inner)
    | proves :putsAnotherTagOverThePayload
        |~~ Another tag goes over the payload taken out. ~~|
        ~(::Box#1 | payload | tag ::Crate | eq ::Crate#1)
    | proves :namesTheSiteOfAnErrorAtRest
        |~~ A tag over an error at rest names its site. ~~|
        ~([::Late !{:by 3}] | tag | eq ::Late!{:by 3})
    | proves :raisesAnOpenedErrorAsItself
        |~~ An opened error split and tagged back raises as itself. ~~|
        ~(1 | add "1" !| get [~(type) ~(payload)] | tag | raise
          !| type
          |  eq ::number/add/addend/KindNotTakenError)
    | proves :name/KindNotTakenError/refusesAKeyword
        |~~ A keyword given as the name is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~(5 | tag :box !| get [~(type) /actualType] | eq [::any/tag/name/KindNotTakenError ::keyword])
    | proves :PairTagNotTagError/refusesAKeyword
        |~~ A keyword names a field and no kind. ~~|
        ~([:foo 42] | tag !| get [~(type) /actualType] | eq [::any/tag/PairTagNotTagError ::keyword])
    | proves :SubjectNotPairError/refusesANumber
        |~~ A number is no pair. ~~|
        ~(42 | tag !| get [~(type) /actualType] | eq [::any/tag/SubjectNotPairError ::number])
    | proves :SubjectNotPairError/refusesAVectorOfThree
        |~~ A vector of three is no pair. ~~|
        ~([::Foo 1 2] | tag !| get [~(type) /actualType] | eq [::any/tag/SubjectNotPairError ::vec])
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesTwoTags
        |~~ Two tags given to one call are refused under the word. ~~|
        ~(5 | tag ::Box ::Crate !| type | eq ::any/tag/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/sumOf
  |~~ Answers the sum of the numbers the call computes against the
      subject, as many as it gives, the subject only what they are read
      from: two fields of one record add by `sumOf /x /y`,
      `{:x 10 :y 3} | sumOf /x /y` answering `13`. A field the record
      lacks answers the refusal of its read, `::path/KeyNotInMapError`,
      and one that may be missing is read with its default,
      `(query /y | coalesce 0)`. Text that holds a
      number is read first by `toNumber`, and the numbers of a vector
      sum by `sum`. The call gives one number at least.
      ::link~(::number/add | doc) ::link~(::vec/sum | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :numbers
        |~~ The numbers to add, one at least, each a number or a path the
            word reads from the subject.
        ~~|
        ~(it) [#[::number ::path]]
    | holds :numbers/EmptyError
        |~~ `sumOf` adds the numbers the call gives, one at least,
            `sumOf /x /y`.
        ~~|
        ~(count | gt 0)
    | throws :numbers/ElementKindNotTakenError
        |~~ Each of the numbers is a number or a path; text that holds one
            is read by `toNumber`, `sumOf (get /x | toNumber) /y`.
        ~~|
    | returns |~~ The sum of the numbers. ~~| ::number
    | proves :addsTwoFields
        |~~ Two fields of one map add. ~~|
        ~({:x 10 :y 3} | sumOf /x /y | eq 13)
    | proves :addsAsManyAsGiven
        |~~ As many fields as the call gives add. ~~|
        ~({:a 1 :b 2 :c 3} | sumOf /a /b /c | eq 6)
    | proves :answersTheErrorOfAMissingField
        |~~ A field the record lacks answers the refusal of its read. ~~|
        ~({:x 1} | sumOf /x /y !| type | eq ::path/KeyNotInMapError)
    | proves :readsAMissingFieldByItsDefault
        |~~ A field that may be missing is read with its default. ~~|
        ~({:x 1} | sumOf /x (query /y | coalesce 0) | eq 1)
    | proves :readsTextByToNumber
        |~~ Text that holds a number is read first by `toNumber`. ~~|
        ~({:x "1" :y 2} | sumOf (get /x | toNumber) /y | eq 3)
    | proves :numbers/EmptyError/refusesNoNumber
        |~~ A call with no number is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~({} | sumOf !| type | eq ::any/sumOf/numbers/EmptyError)
    | proves :numbers/ElementKindNotTakenError/refusesText
        |~~ Text is no number, and is refused at its index. ~~|
        ~({} | sumOf "1"
          !| get [~(type) /index]
          |  eq [::any/sumOf/numbers/ElementKindNotTakenError 0])
    | impl ~(get numbers | sum)

:any/mulOf
  |~~ Answers the product of the factors the call computes against the
      subject, as many as it gives: the price and the quantity of one line
      multiply by `mulOf /price /qty`,
      `{:price 4 :qty 3} | mulOf /price /qty` answering `12`. One factor
      answers itself, and the call gives one at least. Text that holds a
      number is read first by `toNumber`.
      ::link~(::number/mul | doc) ::link~(::any/sumOf | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :factors
        |~~ The numbers multiplied, one at least, each a number or a path
            the word reads from the subject.
        ~~|
        ~(it) [#[::number ::path]]
    | holds :factors/EmptyError
        |~~ `mulOf` multiplies the factors the call gives, one at least,
            `mulOf /price /qty`.
        ~~|
        ~(count | gt 0)
    | throws :factors/ElementKindNotTakenError
        |~~ Each of the factors is a number or a path; text that holds one
            is read by `toNumber`.
        ~~|
    | returns |~~ The factors multiplied. ~~| ::number
    | proves :multipliesAsManyAsGiven
        |~~ As many factors as the call gives multiply. ~~|
        ~({:a 2 :b 3 :c 4} | mulOf /a /b /c | eq 24)
    | proves :answersOneFactor
        |~~ One factor answers itself. ~~|
        ~({:a 7} | mulOf /a | eq 7)
    | proves :factors/EmptyError/refusesNoFactor
        |~~ A call with no factor is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~({:a 2} | mulOf !| type | eq ::any/mulOf/factors/EmptyError)
    | proves :factors/ElementKindNotTakenError/refusesAString
        |~~ A string is no factor, and is refused at its index. ~~|
        ~({:a 2} | mulOf /a "x"
          !| get [~(type) /index]
          |  eq [::any/mulOf/factors/ElementKindNotTakenError 1])
    | proves :multipliesTwoFields
        |~~ The price and the quantity of one line multiply. ~~|
        ~({:price 4 :qty 3} | mulOf /price /qty | eq 12)
    | impl ~(get factors | reduce 1 ~(mul))

:any/subOf
  |~~ Answers the minuend the call computes against the subject less the
      subtrahend: what is left to pay is `subOf /total /paid`,
      `{:total 10 :paid 3} | subOf /total /paid` answering `7`. The call
      gives the two, the minuend first; a sum subtracted is computed in
      its place, `subOf /total (sumOf /a /b)`.
      ::link~(::number/sub | doc) ::link~(::any/sumOf | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :minuend
        |~~ The number subtracted from. ~~|
        /0 #[::number ::path]
    | throws :minuend/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The minuend is a number or a path,
            `{:a 5 :b 3} | subOf /a /b`; text that holds one is read by
            `toNumber` first, `subOf (get /a | toNumber) /b`.
        ~~|
    | takes :subtrahend |~~ The number subtracted. ~~| /1 #[::number ::path]
    | throws :subtrahend/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The subtrahend is a number or a path,
            `{:a 5 :b 3} | subOf /a /b`; text that holds one is read by
            `toNumber` first, `subOf /a (get /b | toNumber)`.
        ~~|
    | throws :subtrahend/NotGivenError
        |~~ `subOf` subtracts the subtrahend from the minuend and was
            given the minuend alone: `subOf /total /paid`.
        ~~|
    | returns |~~ The minuend less the subtrahend. ~~| ::number
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `subOf` subtracts one number from another: a sum subtracted is
            computed in its place, `subOf /total (sumOf /a /b)`.
        ~~|
    | proves :subtractsASumInItsPlace
        |~~ A sum subtracted is computed in its place. ~~|
        ~({:total 10 :a 3 :b 2} | subOf /total (sumOf /a /b) | eq 5)
    | proves :minuend/KindNotTakenError/refusesAString
        |~~ A minuend that is a string is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~({:p 1} | subOf "a" /p
          !| get [~(type) /actualType]
          |  eq [::any/subOf/minuend/KindNotTakenError ::string])
    | proves :subtrahend/NotGivenError/refusesOneNumber
        |~~ A call with the minuend alone is refused under the subtrahend.
        ~~|
        ~({:t 10} | subOf /t !| type | eq ::any/subOf/subtrahend/NotGivenError)
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesAThirdNumber
        |~~ A third number is refused under the word. ~~|
        ~({:t 10 :p 3 :q 1} | subOf /t /p /q !| type | eq ::any/subOf/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | proves :subtrahend/KindNotTakenError/refusesAString
        |~~ A subtrahend that is no number is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~({:a 5} | subOf /a "3" !| type | eq ::any/subOf/subtrahend/KindNotTakenError)
    | proves :subtractsTwoFields
        |~~ What is left to pay is the total less what was paid. ~~|
        ~({:total 10 :paid 3} | subOf /total /paid | eq 7)
    | impl
        ~(get args
          | :pair it
          | (pair | first)
          | sub (pair | last))

:any/divOf
  |~~ Answers the dividend the call computes against the subject divided
      by the divisor: a mean is `divOf /sum /count`,
      `{:sum 20 :count 4} | divOf /sum /count` answering `5`. A quotient
      that falls between whole numbers is a decimal, and a divisor of
      zero is refused as `div` refuses it. The call gives the two, the
      dividend first; a product divided is computed in its place,
      `divOf /total (mulOf /a /b)`.
      ::link~(::number/div | doc) ::link~(::any/mulOf | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :dividend |~~ The number divided. ~~| /0 #[::number ::path]
    | throws :dividend/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The dividend is a number or a path,
            `{:a 6 :b 3} | divOf /a /b`; text that holds one is read by
            `toNumber` first, `divOf (get /a | toNumber) /b`.
        ~~|
    | takes :divisor |~~ The number divided by. ~~| /1 #[::number ::path]
    | throws :divisor/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The divisor is a number or a path,
            `{:a 6 :b 3} | divOf /a /b`; text that holds one is read by
            `toNumber` first, `divOf /a (get /b | toNumber)`.
        ~~|
    | throws :divisor/NotGivenError
        |~~ `divOf` divides the dividend by the divisor and was given the
            dividend alone: `divOf /sum /count`.
        ~~|
    | returns |~~ The dividend divided by the divisor. ~~| ::number
    | throws :ArgsBeyondInputsError
        |~~ `divOf` divides one number by another: a product divided is
            computed in its place, `divOf /total (mulOf /a /b)`.
        ~~|
    | proves :dividesByTheSecond
        |~~ A mean is the sum over the count. ~~|
        ~({:sum 20 :count 4} | divOf /sum /count | eq 5)
    | proves :answersADecimal
        |~~ A quotient between whole numbers is a decimal. ~~|
        ~({:sum 7 :count 2} | divOf /sum /count | eq 3.5)
    | proves :refusesADivisorOfZero
        |~~ A divisor of zero is refused as `div` refuses it. ~~|
        ~({:sum 20 :count 0} | divOf /sum /count !| type | eq ::number/div/divisor/ZeroError)
    | proves :divisor/NotGivenError/refusesOneNumber
        |~~ A call with the dividend alone is refused under the divisor. ~~|
        ~({:s 20} | divOf /s !| type | eq ::any/divOf/divisor/NotGivenError)
    | proves :ArgsBeyondInputsError/refusesAThirdNumber
        |~~ A third number is refused under the word. ~~|
        ~({:s 20 :c 4 :x 1} | divOf /s /c /x !| type | eq ::any/divOf/ArgsBeyondInputsError)
    | proves :dividend/KindNotTakenError/refusesAString
        |~~ A dividend that is no number is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~({:b 3} | divOf "6" /b !| type | eq ::any/divOf/dividend/KindNotTakenError)
    | proves :divisor/KindNotTakenError/refusesAString
        |~~ A divisor that is no number is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~({:a 6} | divOf /a "3" !| type | eq ::any/divOf/divisor/KindNotTakenError)
    | impl
        ~(get args
          | :pair it
          | (pair | first)
          | div (pair | last))

:any/allOf
  |~~ Answers whether every condition the call gives holds of the subject:
      two conditions of one record hold together by
      `allOf (get /active) (get /age | gte 18)`. Each modifier is a
      condition computed at the call, every one of them, or code that
      answers one in its turn, a quote or a path, run on the subject once every condition
      before it held: a condition that fails where an earlier one does not
      hold is written as such a quote,
      `allOf (get /email | ne null) ~(get /email | endsWith ".org")`, and
      the first condition that does not hold answers `false` with the
      quotes after it unrun; a quote that fails answers its error. The
      call gives one condition at least.
      ::link~(::any/anyOf | doc) ::link~(::vec/every | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :conditions
        |~~ The conditions, booleans and quotes that answer them, one at
            least.
        ~~|
        ~(it) [#[::boolean ::path ::quote]]
    | holds :conditions/EmptyError
        |~~ `allOf` asks whether every condition the call gives holds, one
            at least, `allOf (get /active) (get /age | gte 18)`.
        ~~|
        ~(count | gt 0)
    | throws :conditions/ElementKindNotTakenError
        |~~ Each modifier of `allOf` is a condition computed at the call,
            `(get /age | gte 18)`, a path whose field answers one,
            `/active`, or a quote that answers one in its turn,
            `~(get /email | endsWith ".org")`. A condition computed at the
            call is computed at every place, the first among them, and one
            that answered an error is refused here, the error its value: a
            field a record may lack is read softly,
            `allOf (query /b | eq 1)`, and a condition that fails where an
            earlier one does not hold is written as a quote that answers
            it in its turn, which runs only once every condition before it
            held.
        ~~|
    | returns |~~ Whether every condition holds. ~~| ::boolean
    | throws :ConditionNotBooleanError
        |~~ A quote or a path among the conditions of `allOf` answers a
            boolean when it runs; `:index` names, from zero, the condition
            whose code answered another kind, and a quote that reads a
            field compares it to answer a condition,
            `~(get /email | ne null)`.
        ~~|
    | proves :holdsWhenEveryConditionHolds
        |~~ An active adult passes both conditions. ~~|
        ~({:active true :age 25} | allOf (get /active) (get /age | gte 18))
    | proves :failsWhenOneFails
        |~~ A minor fails the second. ~~|
        ~({:active true :age 15} | allOf (get /active) (get /age | gte 18) | not)
    | proves :runsAQuoteInItsTurn
        |~~ A quote runs once the conditions before it held. ~~|
        ~([{:email null} {:email "a@b.org"}]
          | map ~(allOf (get /email | ne null) ~(get /email | endsWith ".org"))
          | eq [false true])
    | proves :answersTheFailureOfAQuote
        |~~ A quote that fails answers its error. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | allOf ~(get /b | eq 1) !| get [~(type) /key] | eq [::path/KeyNotInMapError :b])
    | proves :conditions/ElementKindNotTakenError/refusesAFieldMissingAtTheFirstPlace
        |~~ A field the record lacks is refused at its place, the first
            among them.
        ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | allOf (get /b | eq 1)
          !| get [~(type) /index /actualType]
          |  eq [::any/allOf/conditions/ElementKindNotTakenError 0 ::path/KeyNotInMapError])
    | proves :conditions/ElementKindNotTakenError/readsAMissingFieldSoftly
        |~~ A field the record may lack is read softly. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | allOf (query /b | eq 1) | not)
    | proves :conditions/ElementKindNotTakenError/refusesAConditionThatFailedAtTheCall
        |~~ A condition computed at the call is computed even where an
            earlier one does not hold, and its error is refused at its
            index.
        ~~|
        ~({:age 15} | allOf (get /age | gte 18) (get /licence)
          !| get [~(type) /index /actualType]
          |  eq [::any/allOf/conditions/ElementKindNotTakenError 1 ::path/KeyNotInMapError])
    | proves :conditions/ElementKindNotTakenError/refusesANumber
        |~~ A number is no condition, and is refused at its index. ~~|
        ~({:age 15} | allOf (get /age)
          !| get [~(type) /index /actualType]
          |  eq [::any/allOf/conditions/ElementKindNotTakenError 0 ::number])
    | proves :ConditionNotBooleanError/refusesAQuoteThatAnswersAString
        |~~ A quote that answers a string is refused at its place. ~~|
        ~({:email "a@b.org"} | allOf true /email
          !| get [~(type) /index /actualType]
          |  eq [::any/allOf/ConditionNotBooleanError 1 ::string])
    | proves :ConditionNotBooleanError/holdsAQuoteThatCompares
        |~~ A quote that compares the field it reads answers a condition.
        ~~|
        ~({:email "a@b.org"} | allOf true ~(get /email | ne null))
    | proves :conditions/EmptyError/refusesNoCondition
        |~~ A call with no condition is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~({} | allOf !| type | eq ::any/allOf/conditions/EmptyError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/anyOf
  |~~ Answers whether some condition the call gives holds of the subject:
      a high score passes without the flag,
      `anyOf /vip (get /score | gt 95)`. Each modifier is a condition
      computed at the call or code that answers one in its turn, a quote
      or a path, run on
      the subject once no condition before it held, as for `allOf`: the
      first that holds answers `true` with the quotes after it unrun, so a
      flagged record passes without the field its quote reads,
      `anyOf /vip ~(get /score | gt 95)`, and a quote that fails answers
      its error. The call gives one condition at least.
      ::link~(::any/allOf | doc) ::link~(::vec/any | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :conditions
        |~~ The conditions, booleans and quotes that answer them, one at
            least.
        ~~|
        ~(it) [#[::boolean ::path ::quote]]
    | holds :conditions/EmptyError
        |~~ `anyOf` asks whether some condition the call gives holds, one
            at least, `anyOf (get /vip) (get /score | gt 95)`.
        ~~|
        ~(count | gt 0)
    | throws :conditions/ElementKindNotTakenError
        |~~ Each modifier of `anyOf` is a condition computed at the call,
            `(get /score | gt 95)`, a path whose field answers one,
            `/vip`, or a quote that answers one in its turn,
            `~(get /score | gt 95)`. A condition computed at the call is
            computed at every place, the first among them, and one that
            answered an error is refused here, the error its value: a
            field a record may lack is read softly,
            `anyOf (query /vip | eq true)`, and a condition that fails
            where an earlier one holds is written as a quote that answers
            it in its turn, which runs only once no condition before it
            held.
        ~~|
    | returns |~~ Whether some condition holds. ~~| ::boolean
    | throws :ConditionNotBooleanError
        |~~ A quote or a path among the conditions of `anyOf` answers a
            boolean when it runs; `:index` names, from zero, the condition
            whose code answered another kind, and a quote that reads a
            field compares it to answer a condition,
            `~(get /score | gt 95)`.
        ~~|
    | proves :holdsWhenOneHolds
        |~~ A high score passes without the flag. ~~|
        ~({:vip false :score 99} | anyOf (get /vip) (get /score | gt 95))
    | proves :failsWhenNoneHolds
        |~~ A low score without the flag fails. ~~|
        ~({:vip false :score 50} | anyOf (get /vip) (get /score | gt 95) | not)
    | proves :runsAQuoteInItsTurn
        |~~ A flagged record passes without the field the quote reads. ~~|
        ~([{:vip true} {:vip false :score 99}]
          | map ~(anyOf (get /vip) ~(get /score | gt 95))
          | eq [true true])
    | proves :answersTheFailureOfAQuote
        |~~ A quote that fails answers its error. ~~|
        ~({:vip false} | anyOf (get /vip) ~(get /score | gt 95) !| get [~(type) /key] | eq [::path/KeyNotInMapError :score])
    | proves :conditions/ElementKindNotTakenError/refusesAFieldMissingAtTheFirstPlace
        |~~ A field the record lacks is refused at its place, the first
            among them.
        ~~|
        ~({:score 99} | anyOf (get /vip | eq true)
          !| get [~(type) /index /actualType]
          |  eq [::any/anyOf/conditions/ElementKindNotTakenError 0 ::path/KeyNotInMapError])
    | proves :conditions/ElementKindNotTakenError/readsAMissingFieldSoftly
        |~~ A field the record may lack is read softly. ~~|
        ~({:score 99} | anyOf (query /vip | eq true) (get /score | gt 95))
    | proves :conditions/ElementKindNotTakenError/refusesAConditionThatFailedAtTheCall
        |~~ A condition computed at the call is computed even where an
            earlier one holds, and its error is refused at its index.
        ~~|
        ~({:vip true} | anyOf (get /vip) (get /score | gt 95)
          !| get [~(type) /index /actualType]
          |  eq [::any/anyOf/conditions/ElementKindNotTakenError 1 ::path/KeyNotInMapError])
    | proves :conditions/ElementKindNotTakenError/refusesANumber
        |~~ A number is no condition, and is refused at its index. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | anyOf (get /a)
          !| get [~(type) /index /actualType]
          |  eq [::any/anyOf/conditions/ElementKindNotTakenError 0 ::number])
    | proves :ConditionNotBooleanError/refusesAQuoteThatAnswersANumber
        |~~ A quote that answers a number is refused at its place. ~~|
        ~({:score 99} | anyOf false /score
          !| get [~(type) /index /actualType]
          |  eq [::any/anyOf/ConditionNotBooleanError 1 ::number])
    | proves :ConditionNotBooleanError/holdsAQuoteThatCompares
        |~~ A quote that compares the field it reads answers a condition.
        ~~|
        ~({:score 99} | anyOf false ~(get /score | gt 95))
    | proves :conditions/EmptyError/refusesNoCondition
        |~~ A call with no condition is refused under the input. ~~|
        ~({} | anyOf !| type | eq ::any/anyOf/conditions/EmptyError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/unionOf
  |~~ Answers the union of the parts the call computes against the
      subject, maps or sets, in their order, a later key holding over an
      earlier: the value of a pair with its name beside it is
      `unionOf /1 (get {:name ~(get /0 | toString)})`. Maps unite with maps and sets
      with sets, a map with a set being refused by `union`, and the
      union keeps the tags of the first part. One part answers itself,
      and the call gives one at least.
      ::link~(::map/union | doc) ::link~(::set/union | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :parts |~~ The maps or the sets, one at least. ~~| ~(it)
    | holds :parts/EmptyError
        |~~ `unionOf` unites the parts the call gives, one at least,
            `unionOf /a /b`.
        ~~|
        ~(count | gt 0)
    | returns |~~ The parts united, under the tags of the first. ~~| ::any
    | proves :mergesTwoFields
        |~~ Two maps one value holds merge. ~~|
        ~({:a {:x 1} :b {:y 2}} | unionOf /a /b | eq {:x 1 :y 2})
    | proves :holdsTheLaterKey
        |~~ A later key holds over an earlier. ~~|
        ~({:a {:x 1} :b {:x 2}} | unionOf /a /b | eq {:x 2})
    | proves :foldsTheKeyIntoTheValue
        |~~ The key of a pair joins its value as a field. ~~|
        ~([:auth {:port 3000}] | unionOf /1 (get {:name ~(get /0 | toString)}) | eq {:port 3000 :name "auth"})
    | proves :unitesSets
        |~~ Two sets unite. ~~|
        ~({:a #[1] :b #[2]} | unionOf /a /b | eq #[1 2])
    | proves :refusesAMapWithASet
        |~~ A map with a set is refused by `union`. ~~|
        ~({:a {:x 1} :b #[2]} | unionOf /a /b !| type | eq ::map/union/other/KindNotTakenError)
    | proves :keepsTheTagsOfTheFirst
        |~~ The union keeps the tags of the first part. ~~|
        ~({:a ::Box{:x 1} :b {:y 2}} | unionOf /a /b | eq ::Box{:x 1 :y 2})
    | proves :answersOnePart
        |~~ One part answers itself. ~~|
        ~({:a {:x 1}} | unionOf /a | eq {:x 1})
    | proves :parts/EmptyError/refusesNoPart
        |~~ A call with no part to unite is refused under `parts`. ~~|
        ~({} | unionOf !| type | eq ::any/unionOf/parts/EmptyError)
    | impl ~(get parts | :read it | drop 1 | reduce (read | first) ~(union))

:any/is
  |~~ Answers whether the walk of the subject's tags reaches a value of
      one of the kinds the call gives, `5 | is ::number`. A number under
      a tag of its own is a number, while `type` answers the tag over
      it; a map under a tag is a map; and a set is a vector, as a quote
      and a doc are, while a vector is no set. A failure is asked once
      `!|` catches it, an error at rest, a value of `::error` and of its
      refusal. Given
      several kinds, it answers whether the subject is of one of them,
      and given none, false. A kind no tag of the walk names is not
      reached, so a misspelt kind, `is ::Numbr`, answers false and is
      not refused. It answers and holds nothing: an input of a word is
      held to its kinds by the `takes` that declares it.
      ::link~(::any/type | doc) ::link~(::any/tag | doc)
      ::link~(::fn/takes | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :kinds
        |~~ The kinds asked, each a tag name; of none, no value is one. ~~|
        ~(it) [::tag]
    | throws :kinds/ElementKindNotTakenError
        |~~ Each of the kinds is a tag name, `::number`; a keyword,
            `:number`, names a field and no kind.
        ~~|
    | returns
        |~~ `true` when the walk of the tags reaches one of the kinds,
            `false` otherwise.
        ~~|
        ::boolean
    | proves :holdsOfItsKind
        |~~ A number is a number. ~~|
        ~(5 | is ::number)
    | proves :walksUnderATag
        |~~ A number under a tag of its own is a number. ~~|
        ~(::Width#5 | is ::number)
    | proves :walksUnderATagToAMap
        |~~ A map under a tag is a map. ~~|
        ~(::Box{:a 1} | is ::map)
    | proves :takesASetForAVector
        |~~ A set is a vector. ~~|
        ~(#[1 2] | is ::vec)
    | proves :takesAQuoteAndADocForVectors
        |~~ A quote is a vector of its steps, and a doc of its segments. ~~|
        ~([~(add 1) |~~ A page. ~~|] | map ~(is ::vec) | eq [true true])
    | proves :keepsAVectorApartFromASet
        |~~ A vector is no set. ~~|
        ~([1] | is ::set | not)
    | proves :asksAnOpenedFailure
        |~~ An opened error is a value of `::error` and of its refusal. ~~|
        ~("x" | add 1
          !| get [~(is ::error) ~(is ::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError)]
          |  eq [true true])
    | proves :holdsOfOneOfSeveralKinds
        |~~ Of several kinds, the subject is one of them. ~~|
        ~(5 | is ::string ::number)
    | proves :failsOfOtherKinds
        |~~ A string is neither a number nor null. ~~|
        ~("5" | is ::number ::null | not)
    | proves :failsOfNoKind
        |~~ Of no kinds, no value is one. ~~|
        ~(5 | is | not)
    | proves :failsOfAMisspeltKind
        |~~ A misspelt kind answers false and is not refused. ~~|
        ~(5 | is ::Numbr | not)
    | proves :kinds/ElementKindNotTakenError/refusesAKeyword
        |~~ A keyword is no kind, and is refused at its index. ~~|
        ~(5 | is :number
          !| get [~(type) /index /actualType]
          |  eq [::any/is/kinds/ElementKindNotTakenError 0 ::keyword])
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/get
  |~~ Answers the part of the subject a path names, read strictly: a key
      or a place that is not there is refused, `::path/KeyNotInMapError`
      with the keys near it, so a misspelled field is found where it is
      read, `{:a {:b 1}} | get /a/b` answering `1`. A key the query
      computes is read as the path of one segment, a keyword, a string or
      a number, its text the segment, `get (get /k)`. Over a template, a literal of paths
      and quotes, it answers the template with each path read and each
      quote run against the subject in its place, the answer written in
      the shape the template takes:
      `{:price 4 :qty 3} | get [/price /qty]` answers `[4 3]`, and
      `get {:who /first :n ~(get /tags | count)}` builds a map. A vector, a
      map or a set under a tag in a template is read and laid back under
      its tag through its constructor, and any other value of it is
      answered as it is, a quote under a tag, a doc, a function and a kind among them. A literal computes nothing, so a shape computed
      is written as a template. A part that may be missing is read by
      `query`.
      ::link~(::any/query | doc) ::link~(::path | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :path
        |~~ The path read, a key of one segment, or a template of paths
            and quotes.
        ~~|
        /0
    | proves :readsANestedPart
        |~~ A path reads a part at any depth. ~~|
        ~({:a {:b [10 20]}} | get /a/b/-1 | eq 20)
    | proves :refusesAMissedKey
        |~~ A key that is not there is refused. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | get /b !| type | eq ::path/KeyNotInMapError)
    | proves :readsAKeyTheQueryComputes
        |~~ A key the query computes reads as the path of one segment. ~~|
        ~({:k "b" :b 7} | get (get /k) | eq 7)
    | proves :readsATuple
        |~~ Two fields of one record are read in one step. ~~|
        ~({:price 4 :qty 3} | get [/price /qty] | eq [4 3])
    | proves :buildsTheShapeOfItsTemplate
        |~~ A template of paths and quotes builds the answer in its shape. ~~|
        ~({:first "Ann" :tags [:a :b]} | get {:who /first :n ~(get /tags | count)} | eq {:who "Ann" :n 2})
    | proves :readsUnderATagOfTheTemplate
        |~~ A vector under a tag in a template is read and laid back under
            its tag.
        ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | get [::Box[/a]] | eq [::Box[1]])
    | proves :answersAFunctionOfTheTemplateAsItIs
        |~~ A function in a template is answered as it is. ~~|
        ~(get [::fn[~(takes :by |~~ How much. ~~| /0) ~(add by)]] | first | type | eq ::fn)
    | proves :readsBeneathTheTags
        |~~ A path reads beneath the tags over the subject. ~~|
        ~(::Box{:w 3} | get /w | eq 3)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/query
  |~~ Answers the part of the subject a path names, read softly: where a
      key or a place is missing, or null stands on the way, it answers
      null, or the default the call gives, `{:a 1} | query /b 0`
      answering `0`, while a null that stands at the path is answered as
      it stands. A value on the path that has no parts is refused as
      `get` refuses it, so a path read from the wrong subject is found.
      Over a template it answers the template with each path read softly
      and each quote run, `{:a 1} | query [/a /b]` answering `[1 null]`.
      ::link~(::any/get | doc) ::link~(::any/coalesce | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :path
        |~~ The path read, a key of one segment, or a template of paths
            and quotes.
        ~~|
        /0
    | takes :default
        |~~ What a missed path answers, held and never run, null when the
            call gives none.
        ~~|
        ~(query /1 null)
    | proves :answersNullForAMissingKey
        |~~ A key that is not there answers null. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | query /b | eq null)
    | proves :answersTheDefault
        |~~ A missed path answers the default the call gives. ~~|
        ~([] | query /0 7 | eq 7)
    | proves :answersANullThatStands
        |~~ A null at the path is answered as it stands. ~~|
        ~([null] | query /0 7 | eq null)
    | proves :readsThroughNull
        |~~ A null on the way answers null. ~~|
        ~({:a null} | query /a/b | eq null)
    | proves :refusesAnotherKind
        |~~ A value on the path that has no parts is refused. ~~|
        ~({:a 5} | query /a/b !| type | eq ::path/SubjectHasNoPartsError)
    | proves :readsATupleSoftly
        |~~ Each path of a template is read softly. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | query [/a /b] | eq [1 null])
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/put
  |~~ Answers the subject with a value laid at the path the call gives:
      a key the last map on the path lacks is added, and a key it holds or
      a place a vector holds is replaced, `{:a {:b 1}} | put /a/c 2`
      answering `{:a {:b 1 :c 2}}`. The path is followed as `get` follows
      it, so a key or a place missing on the way is refused where the
      path writes it, and a value under a tag on the way is laid under it
      again, its constructor and its invariants run. A key the query
      computes is the path of one segment, `put :a 1`, and a vector grows
      by `append`.
      ::link~(::any/update | doc) ::link~(::any/delete | doc)
      ::link~(::any/get | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :path
        |~~ The path written, or a key of one segment. ~~|
        /0 #[::keyword ::number ::path ::string]
    | throws :path/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The path is a path, `put /a/b 2`, or a key of one segment, a
            keyword, a string or a number, `put :a 2`.
        ~~|
    | takes :value |~~ The value laid at the path. ~~| /1
    | proves :addsAKey
        |~~ A key the last map lacks is added. ~~|
        ~({:a {:b 1}} | put /a/c 2 | eq {:a {:b 1 :c 2}})
    | proves :replacesAPlace
        |~~ A place a vector holds is replaced. ~~|
        ~([1 2 3] | put /-1 9 | eq [1 2 9])
    | proves :refusesAKeyMissedOnTheWay
        |~~ A key missing on the way is refused where the path writes it.
        ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | put /b/c 2 !| get [~(type) /path] | eq [::path/KeyNotInMapError /b/c])
    | proves :refusesAPlacePastTheEnd
        |~~ A vector grows by `append`, not by a place past its end. ~~|
        ~([1 2] | put /2 3 !| type | eq ::path/IndexOutOfBoundsError)
    | proves :laysTheTagsAgain
        |~~ A value under a tag is laid under it again. ~~|
        ~(::Box{:w 2} | put /w 3 | eq ::Box{:w 3})
    | proves :laysEveryTagAgain
        |~~ A value under two tags is laid under both again. ~~|
        ~(::A::B{:w 2} | put /w 3 | eq ::A::B{:w 3})
    | proves :refusesAValueWithoutParts
        |~~ A value on the path that has no parts is refused, as `get`
            refuses it.
        ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | put /a/b 2 !| type | eq ::path/SubjectHasNoPartsError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/update
  |~~ Answers the subject with the part at the path the call gives laid
      anew by what the code the call gives answers for it,
      `{:n 1} | update /n ~(add 1)` answering `{:n 2}`. The part must be
      there, as for `get`, an error the code answers is the answer, and
      the tags on the way are laid again as `put` lays them.
      ::link~(::any/put | doc) ::link~(::tagged/within | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :path
        |~~ The path written, or a key of one segment. ~~|
        /0 #[::keyword ::number ::path ::string]
    | throws :path/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The path is a path, `update /n ~(add 1)`, or a key of one
            segment, `update :n ~(add 1)`.
        ~~|
    | takes :code
        |~~ The code run on the part, a quote or a path. ~~|
        /1 #[::path ::quote]
    | throws :code/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The code is a quote, `update /n ~(add 1)`, or a path,
            `update /n /value`; written without the tilde,
            `update /n (add 1)`, it is computed once at the call and
            refused.
        ~~|
    | proves :runsTheCodeOnThePart
        |~~ The part at the path is laid anew by what the code answers. ~~|
        ~({:a {:n 1}} | update /a/n ~(add 1) | eq {:a {:n 2}})
    | proves :refusesAMissedPart
        |~~ A part that is not there is refused. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | update /b ~(add 1) !| type | eq ::path/KeyNotInMapError)
    | proves :answersTheErrorOfItsCode
        |~~ An error the code answers is the answer. ~~|
        ~({:n "x"} | update /n ~(add 1) !| type | eq ::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError)
    | proves :answersTheErrorBeneathTheTags
        |~~ An error the code answers beneath a tag is the answer, laid
            under none.
        ~~|
        ~(::A::B{:n "x"} | update /n ~(add 1) !| type | eq ::number/add/SubjectNotNumberError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/delete
  |~~ Answers the subject without the part at the path the call gives: a
      key taken out of a map, or a place out of a vector, the elements
      after it moving up, `{:a 1 :b 2} | delete /b` answering `{:a 1}`.
      The part must be there, as for `get`, so a misspelled key is found;
      keys that may be absent leave a map by `minus`. The tags on the way
      are laid again as `put` lays them.
      ::link~(::any/put | doc) ::link~(::map/minus | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :path
        |~~ The path of the part taken out, or a key of one segment. ~~|
        /0 #[::keyword ::number ::path ::string]
    | throws :path/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The path is a path, `delete /a/b`, or a key of one segment,
            `delete :a`.
        ~~|
    | proves :takesAKeyOut
        |~~ A key is taken out of the map at the path. ~~|
        ~({:a {:b 1 :c 2}} | delete /a/b | eq {:a {:c 2}})
    | proves :takesAPlaceOut
        |~~ A place is taken out of a vector, the elements after it moving
            up.
        ~~|
        ~([1 2 3] | delete /0 | eq [2 3])
    | proves :refusesAMissedKey
        |~~ A key that is not there is refused. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | delete /b !| type | eq ::path/KeyNotInMapError)
    | impl ::builtin{}

:any/it
  |~~ Answers the value in the pipe, the subject itself: `:r it` names
      the record a later step reads back, `get [~(it) ~(count)]` holds it beside
      what it counts, and `5 | mul it` squares it.
      ::link~(::qlang/names | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | proves :answersTheSubject
        |~~ The subject itself multiplies the subject. ~~|
        ~(5 | mul it | eq 25)
    | proves :namesTheRecord
        |~~ A name keeps the record for a later step. ~~|
        ~({:a 1} | :r it | get /a | add (r | get /a) | eq 2)
    | impl ~()

:any/ok
  |~~ Answers `true`. A step after a failure is skipped, so `ok !| false`
      answers whether the step before it stood: `5 | ok !| false`
      answers `true`, and a failure before it `false`.
      ::link~(::qlang/errors | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | proves :asksWhetherTheStepBeforeStood
        |~~ `ok !| false` tells a value from a failure. ~~|
        ~(get [~(5 | ok !| false) ~(!{:k 1} | raise | ok !| false)] | eq [true false])
    | impl ~(true)

:any/paths
  |~~ Answers the set of the paths at which the value holds no parts, the
      outline of any value, each a path `get` reads:
      `{:a {:b 1 :c [2 3]}} | paths` answers `#[/a/b /a/c/0 /a/c/1]`. An
      empty vector or map is a part that holds none, and a value under a
      tag is read beneath it.
      ::link~(::any/get | doc) ::link~(::path | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | returns |~~ The paths of the parts that hold no parts. ~~| ::set
    | proves :answersTheOutline
        |~~ The outline holds the path of every part that holds no parts.
        ~~|
        ~({:a {:b 1 :c [2 3]} :d []} | paths | map ~(print) | eq ["/a/b" "/a/c/0" "/a/c/1" "/d"])
    | proves :answersNoPathOfAValueWithoutParts
        |~~ A value that has no parts has no path in it. ~~|
        ~(5 | paths | eq #[])
    | proves :readsBeneathTheTags
        |~~ The outline reads beneath the tags stacked over a value. ~~|
        ~(::A::B{:w 2} | paths | map ~(print) | eq ["/w"])
    | proves :eachReadsByGet
        |~~ Each path of the outline reads its part. ~~|
        ~({:a {:b 1}} | :v it | paths | map ~(:p it | (v | get p)) | eq [1])
    | impl ::builtin{}

